WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Punch Out! Meet the Ultimate Challenge Fight!

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:26.720
Mr. Dream. And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:27.880
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.200
I'm Sam. I'm Joe.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:34.380
And I'm Sean. And today we're talking about, if you haven't guessed it from the double intro, Punch Out.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.080
Guys, what do we know about Mr.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.180
Dream? I feel like we've learned a lot recently by playing the game.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:48.320
You know, I know that he was a very controversial boxing man who threatened to eat people's children.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.200
And eventually like ate someone's ear.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.500
Eats ears. Yeah, bit an ear.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.260
Stars in the Hangover.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.240
I feel like that's how I know him the best.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:00.760
Yeah. Because I had never watched boxing or anything, but I did watch The Hangover a lot when it came out.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.440
Yeah, and he was a big deal.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.420
And what's interesting is Nintendo signed Mr.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.080
Dream to this deal before he even became like the world boxing champion.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.100
So that was a good get for them, I guess.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:18.060
They just said, I think it was someone from Nintendo of America went to a boxing fight, saw Mr.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.620
Dream. This guy's a big deal.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:20.600
Him. Yeah, he's a big deal.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.120
Like, let's get him a contract.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.080
And then they signed him and six months later he became the world boxing champion.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:27.960
So good get for Nintendo.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.860
Yeah. You're right to say that he's a controversial figure now.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.380
But at the time he was the biggest deal in sports.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.860
I mean, you had Jordan in basketball.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.260
You had Gretzky on the ice.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.380
Who's like the baseball equivalent?

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.200
What game was on ice?

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.240
Ken Griffey, I guess.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:44.300
Ken Griffey is all I was going to say.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.300
Wait, were you saying Ken Griffey on ice?

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:47.880
No, no, no. It's a musical.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.420
Oh, what? The sport on ice?

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.040
That's hockey? Oh, yes.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.540
We haven't gotten a hockey game yet on the NES.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:53.400
I know, I'm looking forward to one.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.260
Is that what you were alluding to?

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.180
No, I just wanted to make a joke.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.740
Okay. Got it.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.960
Was there any football equivalent to those other sports?

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.340
I guess like Bo, but I don't think...

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:05.480
But he was also the baseball guy, right?

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:06.660
I guess he was also baseball.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.260
Yeah, that's interesting. Jim Kelly.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.000
Specifically and only Jim Kelly.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.020
Shout out Buffalo.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.080
And Thurman Thomas.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:15.700
Anyway. Anyway, yeah.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.240
So, had any of you guys played Punch-Out before this record?

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:20.980
It's weird because this...

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.240
Well, before this record, yes.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:23.480
That was a true question.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.460
I got you.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.580
No, it's weird because this is like one of those games where it's like, of course I know

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:35.140
about it, but I just always assumed it's one of those games that everyone has this crazy

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:36.960
nostalgia for, but doesn't really hold up.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:43.400
So, this was the first time I've played it and I was pleasantly surprised that it was

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.380
not quite what I was thinking it was.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.100
Well, you know, that's the thing with the NES games is a lot of them, as Sam would say,

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:54.960
they're janky. You know, it's like they used to be good games, but now over time they just haven't quite matured.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.520
Yeah. But this one feels pretty good.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:58.940
Would you guys agree? For sure.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:03.620
This was my first time playing any Punch-Out game, so it was kind of...

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.540
It was weird because it was new, but it was also exactly what I expected.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:15.300
Huh. Okay. I would say that, yeah, Punch-Out was always just a game that sort of in the same mold

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:20.840
as... What was the game that was in that we have already played?

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:22.520
Ranking? No.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.320
The game that was featured in Back to the Future.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.460
Oh, Hogan's Alley.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.140
Okay. No. The other one.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.220
Urban Champion?

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:31.860
The Western one.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.300
Oh, wow, yeah.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:34.440
I'm thinking Hogan's Alley.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.400
Yeah, you're thinking of...

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.200
Oh, shoot. We did a whole podcast about this.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.620
Wild Gunman? Wild Gunman.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:46.620
In the same mold as Wild Gunman, it's just the iconography and the imagery of the game

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:52.740
are plastered into our cultural mind because we've seen that movie and we've seen it played

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.040
in other places, but it was a game I never played.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.700
That was sort of what Punch-Out was to me.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.280
I just knew the wireframe for Super Punch-Out.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:05.660
I had played Super Punch-Out very briefly before this one, only because I have a Super NES classic.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.740
And we all know Little Mac from Super Smash Bros.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:09.700
From Super Smash Bros., yeah.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:10.860
Which is an interesting take.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.940
Yeah. So, yeah, this is my first time playing the original.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.300
Yeah. And so I mentioned Punch-Out started as an arcade game.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.700
Genyo Takata produced that.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:27.880
Miyamoto actually designed the characters, including Little Mac, although in the arcade version, Little Mac is a wireframe character.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:31.420
He's not a small little guy, so I guess you can't even call him Little Mac.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.280
They don't call him that in the arcade game.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.160
Mecha Mac. Yeah.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:43.860
Wire Mac. So there's that, but also there's like a split-screen version, or the only version of the arcade game.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:45.440
It plays on two separate TVs.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:53.760
So there's your TV that you look at where all the boxing match is happening, and then there's another TV above that where all the stats and the meters are.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.900
And it's kind of interesting that they could, you know, it's almost like a DS look to it, where there's a top and bottom screen.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:05.620
Dual screen. So if you wanted to play that, you know, on MAME or something, you would need to turn your monitor like vertically.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:06.880
Interesting. Yeah.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.960
Not many games have that going for them outside of shmups.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:11.620
A lot of shmups do vertical, but.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:20.500
Oh, yeah. So Takata was known for Popeye and StarTropics, but this game is something that he produced before it ever got big on the NES.

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.400
Is StarTropics a game that comes out in North America?

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.940
Yes. And it only comes out in North America.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.300
I think that's right.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.840
I remember. I think it came out in Europe too, to be honest.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:38.300
But the arcade version has Little Mac as a wireframe character, and he's unnamed, and Koji Kondo did all the music for the game.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:41.320
So that's pretty cool, but it's also his first game as a composer.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:47.200
So we've already been introduced to him through Legend of Zelda and Super Mario Brothers, but Punch-Out was where he got his start.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.200
Sam, any music thoughts on this game?

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.420
Sam, music is good in this game.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:51.520
I'm a big fan.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:06:00.360
Joe? It gives you like, it's really just the one track while you're fighting, but it gives you like an on the edge of your seat sort of feel.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.780
It really gives you attention.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:04.760
It's some pump-up music, for sure.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:11.700
Yeah. And this was a Nintendo R&D 3 joint, which we don't really ever talk about them too much.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.720
It's usually R&D 1 or R&D 4.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:17.000
So for R&D 3 to come in here and hit something is interesting.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:21.980
Their primary responsibilities were for technical hardware and design.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:30.300
So I guess that's how the Punch-Out, it just seems like, oh, if you're going to develop this thing for two TVs, might as well have the R&D 3 division do the hardware and the software part of it.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.080
So there's that.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.860
But more importantly, R&D 3 was also credited with the creation of the backup memory battery to save NES games.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:42.480
Obviously, the first implementation of that being The Legend of Zelda, which we just played a few podcasts ago.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:46.060
Did this game have that for saving records?

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.160
It did not.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:55.380
And I think that's interesting because it came out in the United States after Zelda, but they just chose not to include a battery on it.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.800
But the password system is pretty good.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.340
I think it's because there's not too much for it to remember.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:02.340
It's either where were you in the...

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:03.520
And what's your record.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:05.400
Yeah, exactly. So it's not like there's...

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:10.500
Whereas Metroid had a very long password that you had to fill in each time, and that was a little teeny.

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:13.420
Yeah, you don't have like an inventory or anything that needs to remember or anything like that.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:17.620
We haven't played it, but I know Castlevania 2 has one of the longest ever.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:22.060
Oh, no. Yeah, it's like something like 30 characters.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:25.900
So, okay, so that's that on the arcade game.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:27.320
We are not here to talk about the arcade game.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:28.700
We're here to talk about the NES game.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:33.000
I figured we'd do a little Tales from the Manual section here just to kind of give the story.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:34.600
You play as Little Mac.

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:38.440
He was born in the Bronx, which is in New York City for those that don't know.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:39.900
17 years old.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.460
107 pounds. Who let this kid in the ring?

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:52.820
You commented on this earlier, Sean, about how, you know, he's fighting Piston Honda in there, and that guy is just this mammoth of a man.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:58.180
And then you have this 107 pound little boy in the ring, and the ref's like, he's not even 18 yet.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.800
The thing is, Mike, that I don't think I told you that.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:03.200
That might have been Joe. Oh, okay.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:08.400
But it's just that whole idea of kids like you.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.200
Like, this kid's just like you.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:11.960
Yeah. It's an underdog story.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:18.020
Yeah. Because really, most of the enemies, not all of them, but most of them are at least more than twice your size.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:19.920
Like, you're as big as their feet.

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:23.380
And I'm not sure if you guys know this, but I'll go ahead and spoil it.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.040
That was obviously done because they couldn't recreate the wireframe in the NES version.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:36.400
So they made him small so that this way there was a way to see what your opponent was doing without, like, that's the whole reason he was wireframe in the arcade version.

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:40.220
So you could see through him. So in the arcade version, he was like an appropriately sized person.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:41.300
Appropriately sized person, yes.

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:43.600
Yes. And so in this one, they make him very small.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:46.860
So this way it's, I like the theory that he's you.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:48.480
Yeah. If you're small, you're a kid.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.940
You can beat Hippo King.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:53.840
Oh, God. That's not a good memory.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.860
But yeah, so in this one, he's just small.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:09:04.160
And I like that they use that as a design choice because his reaches when he goes for uppercuts, it's really cool how it translates.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.980
It doesn't feel like Michael Jordan extending his arm in Space Jam.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:11.100
You know, it actually looks like the arm connects.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.320
Yeah. So that's that.

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:16.580
And there's not much of a story to the game other than you're just kind of working through the circuits.

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:22.420
But there is that Doc Lewis character who, you know, who gives you advice.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:30.220
Yeah. And, you know, it's weird to think that he's like some 40 year old man who found this 17 year old kid.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:34.740
He's a phenom. Of course, he's going to find some attention if he can fight with the big guys.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:37.400
I can't really stop thinking about the Rocky parallels.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:40.460
Right. There's a lot of Rocky going on in this punch out thing.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:41.720
There is. And Rocky was 1979.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:49.440
Honestly, I thought that it was going to actually take place in Philadelphia because they're in between matches or in between circuits.

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:50.700
I don't remember. Yeah. Circuits.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:57.340
There will be these little cut scenes of him basically doing the Rocky run like from the montages.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:59.120
But then you see the Statue of Liberty.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.580
So I guess that ties in with the Bronx.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.180
But I just thought, you know, I thought they could throw it back to Rocky.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:11.060
And since we're in the New York City thing and we're talking about that, Sam, you had a little thought about those cut scenes.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:26.740
Well, we were talking about where that takes place because when you see originally I was thinking that he was like somewhere on the Upper West Side looking at looking at Newark across across the river, you know, and then I realized, no, he's not.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:32.500
He's not there. So but the Statue of Liberty comes from the left side, which means he's going south.

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:35.020
He is going south. So worth Mike and I were talking.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.160
We think he's Staten Island.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:41.620
Hey, I think he's in Staten Island, but I still don't see why he wouldn't see the Twin Towers.

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:46.060
So the thing is that we're now going to debate about the geography of Punch-Out.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:47.160
Yeah, based on where it is. Well, I'll tell you one thing.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:49.540
Even though he was born in the Bronx, he's definitely not in the Bronx.

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:50.780
No, he's not. Of course not.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:55.940
For the moments where I thought he was running on the Upper West Side, I thought it was really cool because that's where I run.

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:58.820
And I see a similar view when I'm running.

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:00.280
I don't see the Statue of Liberty.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:01.440
A picturesque Newark.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:03.000
He's just a 17-year-old boy like me.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:03.920
Right, exactly. Yeah.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:05.460
No, I thought that was cool.

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:07.880
And so we started talking about it and I realized no.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:10.680
Now it's a dog shit game because of poor...

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:12.900
I did think it was weird that they didn't include like a map.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:14.960
There should be a map in the manual of...

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:20.660
Yeah, I thought it was weird that you don't get a choice either of your signing bonuses and you go to the next world circuits.

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:23.320
There's no money that you can use from your championships.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:24.480
Yeah, it's very unrealistic.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.500
I would like to...

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:33.860
I don't know if this is a tangent, but I would like to state that I like the framing of this entire game.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:47.080
I like it when games create a story out of just the structure of sports, whether it's like a franchise mode or season mode or going through a circuit.

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:48.000
I like it.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:49.180
That... It's sort of...

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:56.400
The small stories that this game presents and they're very small stories fit really well into that sort of mold.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:12:06.400
But even with the other characters, because like you'll beat Piston Honda and he was the original champion of the first circuit and then he shows up two circuits later and he's a lot better too.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:09.940
So there's even like a story there about how even your villains like...

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:16.040
Villains. You know, it's like referring to like Mark McGuire as like a villain of baseball, right?

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:18.840
Like... So no, they're not villains.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:25.640
But the other fighters, they, you know, they've worked up too to get better and develop new strategies and stuff like that.

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:30.560
They could have easily just taken Piston Honda again and just had his timing be better.

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:33.660
But they even implement like new moves for him and stuff, which is cool.

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:38.600
Yeah. And then there's that limited move set for Little Mac.

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:43.100
You know, he's got jabs, body blows, the uppercut, and then that star special.

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:48.720
Doesn't sound like a lot, but it really works effectively just giving you that limited range.

00:12:48.920 --> 00:13:00.880
Oh, yeah. I think that the simplicity of it allows for a lot of creativity in how you go about creating your combos or just discovering what works on what character.

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:08.000
Yeah, because it doesn't feel like when you start, it doesn't feel like a game that you would have to use a lot of strategy or really thinking about it.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.360
You would think you're just trying to hit him as much as you can.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:15.800
But it's really, it's really a lot of like learning and strategizing for each opponent.

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:17.320
Yeah. It's almost like a puzzle game.

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:18.600
Exactly what I was going to say.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:22.180
Yeah. This game is a puzzle game masquerading as a sports title.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:31.540
Yeah. Tricked you. Yeah. And the best, Sam and I have both followed the speed running, and the best players in the world, for them, it's now, it's still like a dexterity battle.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:37.640
Like it's getting the inputs in fast enough so that they can shave off the slightest milliseconds of their time.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:43.800
Because if they can just get that punch in a beat earlier, because the game actually is timed to the millisecond.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:50.640
So if they can get that punch in just a millisecond earlier, it'll count and it'll get a different result than whatever would have happened next.

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:53.320
Wow. There's very little RNG in the game.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:55.600
I think certain enemies have some RNG.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:57.440
I think Mr. Dream is a lot in this fight.

00:13:57.620 --> 00:14:01.340
So there's that, but at the same time, there's the tells.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.460
And the tells are the most interesting part of the game for me.

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:11.580
Every character has their own tell that they're about to punch you, which is great because their punches do an awful lot of damage.

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:13.720
So it's much better to know.

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:15.760
I think of this in a shmup, right?

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:24.620
Wouldn't it be great if every ship that you were seeing ahead of you communicated that it was about to charge at you or shoot at you rather than just act randomly?

00:14:24.620 --> 00:14:28.860
And that's kind of what these fighters do is they communicate, I'm about to punch you.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:30.700
And you think like, oh, that sounds really easy.

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:32.120
He's going to tell me every single time.

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:34.280
But you have to be in rhythm with him.

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:37.000
Yeah. So it's as much a puzzle game as it is a rhythm game.

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:42.100
You're right. Any other thoughts on that timing and telling mechanic?

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:43.900
Because I think the tell is something that we...

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:45.620
Well, that is the defining...

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:54.460
That is what separates each level of the game is just what pattern they have, what tell you'll have to...

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:55.700
They communicate to you.

00:14:56.420 --> 00:15:00.340
So without them, this is just a series of the same match over and over.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.600
So yeah, I think it's the most important part of the fighting mechanics.

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:08.000
Yeah. It reminds me a little bit of something like Monster Hunter.

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:12.280
Oh, yeah. You know, where the thing that you're trying to kill has...

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:18.440
You know, it has a certain tell to it that you have to figure out and exploit and figure out how to get around that thing.

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:21.720
And even when you think you've figured it out, a new one gets added.

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:32.420
Yeah. So there's this whole thing of when you think you've figured out Piston Honda, the champion for the first circuit, all of a sudden he takes a step back and he starts charging up for something.

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:34.500
And, you know, I've never seen this before.

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:37.540
What's about to happen? And then he goes left, right, left, right, left, right.

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:40.560
And there's many strategies to how to work around that.

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:46.380
Sam showed me a really cool one right before we started the show where you give him a belly punch at the perfect time to knock him out.

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:47.380
And he goes, oh, my tummy.

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:49.220
He does. He really does say that.

00:15:49.220 --> 00:15:51.260
He says that verbatim, yeah.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:56.700
In Japanese. No. What's funny is we have three different stories here.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:57.800
He says it in English.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:59.060
He says it in Japanese.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:00.100
He says it at all.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:03.380
I know the answer, but I'm just not saying it.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:06.780
Okay, good. I was just so excited to perform it that I wasn't listening to it.

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:11.040
That's cool. I mean, there is some voice synthesis in this game, right?

00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:16.620
I don't have a recollection of any actual voice synthesis.

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:22.880
Okay. I thought the referee did something with the timing, but, you know, when he's counting out.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:25.600
But maybe I'm mixing memories with the arcade.

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:27.340
Does he say TKO?

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:29.780
He might say TKO.

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:32.480
I have no. Oh, yeah, but it's not TKO.

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:33.580
He's like, merr, merr, merr.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:35.160
Oh, yeah. Okay, yeah.

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:39.160
And for the record, don't talk to me like, oh, you mean Mario?

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:40.800
You mean Mario? You've never heard of him?

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:43.080
They never established that that is Mario.

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:44.060
It's obviously Mario.

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:45.180
I heard that they snuck him in.

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:46.400
That he wasn't.

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:48.300
Nobody noticed. No, that he wasn't approved that he was Mario.

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:52.180
You think it's just another, like, generic, short Italian man?

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:53.640
Well, he shows up in everything.

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:56.180
He was the golfer in golf.

00:16:57.120 --> 00:16:59.280
That one's still a little bit of a stretch for me, the golfer.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:00.280
And it turns out not to be real.

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:05.700
Remember, we talked about that obscure Japanese Wii game that gives that golf character a totally different persona.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:07.640
You're telling me right now that Mario is not the referee.

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:10.460
No, I'm sure he is, but he was also the umpire in tennis.

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:13.280
So how many times does this guy have between saving the princess?

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:19.180
The thing about Mario is he has all the time in the world between saving the princess times.

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:20.820
Yeah, what is he? He's kind of waiting around.

00:17:20.820 --> 00:17:22.140
Is he only saving princesses?

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:23.180
There's a problem. These are his weekends.

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:26.540
Yeah. Well, it is in New York City, so at least that makes sense.

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:28.080
There's no...

00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:32.220
I mean, maybe he was playing at the US Open for the tennis thing, and that's why he was the umpire.

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:36.720
But to Sam's point, you mentioned that he was kind of snuck into the game.

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:38.000
I heard that somewhere. Is that true?

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:44.560
I believe it just because Miyamoto had this thing where whenever there was a chance to throw in Mario in the early days,

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:47.340
he would just be like, oh, we can make Mario that, or we can put Mario here.

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:52.580
There was a whole thing for Super Mario Brothers where that game was originally just a...

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:54.300
They were just going to have Mario. Yeah, seriously.

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:57.460
Are you serious? Yeah, they didn't know what they were going to do with it.

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:02.600
They were just designing this big game, and then they were like, well, Mario Brothers is selling really well.

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:04.460
Let's put Super Mario Brothers...

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:10.500
For some reason, I was imagining like it was Super Mario Brothers 2, and I was like, should we even put Mario in there?

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:12.260
Mario is missing.

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:37.820
Yeah. I think Punch-Out!

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:40.460
works best by what it doesn't let you control.

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:42.120
Does that make sense?

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:44.700
Yeah. Like, you can't control his movement.

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:46.900
Yeah, and remember Ring King?

00:18:47.320 --> 00:18:49.240
I don't know if it was just a human meat thing.

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:50.240
The classic Ring King?

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:52.180
Yeah, the BJ thing.

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:53.360
Oh. Is that the BJ game?

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:54.960
Well, yeah, that was in that.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:56.800
Defining characteristic of the game.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:59.420
Yeah. What does that say about that game?

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:00.840
That's the first thing you think of.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:03.080
But anyway, Ring King, that was a boxing game.

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.480
Yeah. And you get to do everything you could possibly do in boxing.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:08.220
You pivot around. You can clinch.

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:12.120
And I don't know about you, Sean, but for me, that made a very clunky experience.

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:14.040
It was a terrible game. Yeah, that didn't do anything.

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:18.860
Whereas in this one, because they've decided you shouldn't have to move, you could duck

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:22.580
out of the way, but you shouldn't have to move, now all of a sudden you have to focus

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:28.060
more on the punches. And because you only have two different types of punches and then left and right choosing

00:19:28.060 --> 00:19:31.500
your sides, now you don't have many ways to control your punch, so you don't have to

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:33.320
worry about not connecting those punches.

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:35.120
It's either going to be blocked or it's going to punch.

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:39.700
Right. I think the limited thing here, the simplicity of it, is actually what complements it the

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:42.000
most. Yeah. I think that works in a lot of games.

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:46.120
Go ahead. I think in developing it, it made it a lot easier for them.

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:50.700
I imagine it would make it a lot easier for them to create something where they can control

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:54.240
what strategy you're supposed to be trying to figure out.

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:56.460
So they're figuring out, this is what we need them to do.

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:59.600
We need them to think to hit this weak spot and that weak spot.

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:02.920
But if you have too many things that you're able to do where you're thinking, I'll just run

00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:05.540
to this side of the ring or dodge this way.

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:10.540
It kind of lends itself to having specific strategies that you're supposed to discover.

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:17.640
For sure. I think it also is just another example of the prevailing theme so far in the NES catalog

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:22.680
of simulation sports games really having a hard time being good.

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:28.320
And when they actually go in a more arcade route, you can have a lot more fun with it

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:31.960
because you don't have to worry about micromanaging all the simulation aspects of it.

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:37.440
Yeah. I mean, imagine if all of a sudden, let's just say you could hold on to your opponent.

00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:40.460
You know, in boxing, sometimes they'll put their arms on the other guy's shoulder.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:42.080
Imagine if that was like an option.

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:44.220
Forget that there's not an input for that, right?

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:45.640
Like you wouldn't even know how to map that.

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:50.880
But that's a whole other thing now that you have to add to the game for each character to work a separate way.

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:54.260
And so by doing that, you're just complicating the game further than it needs to be.

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:59.800
I also think even just imagine if you could punch the chest instead of just the body or the head.

00:21:00.020 --> 00:21:05.200
You know, it'd be nice, but you don't have to have it either because it's perfectly fine without it.

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:06.780
I think simplicity is king in this game.

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:09.500
Yeah. I think in every game, honestly.

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:11.660
Oh, okay. Yeah. Especially for these older games.

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:16.320
I agree with that. Especially when the limitations of the system are only so much.

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:18.060
So you work well within that.

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:23.300
One of my favorite games on the system so far, Balloon Fight, is one of the most simple games that we've ever played.

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:25.600
Yeah. Anybody can pick that game up.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:29.320
And I don't even think you really need instructions.

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:31.300
No. You just start pressing the button.

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:36.340
So speaking of instructions, I think that's going to get us into Glass Joe, who we can finally talk about.

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:37.420
Not Joe over here.

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:42.100
Glass Joe is the first enemy you fight in the minor circuit.

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:44.340
And he's not really a tutorial.

00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:47.700
You know, we've seen things where Super Mario Brothers has like that.

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:49.940
He's the closest thing this game gets to one.

00:21:50.020 --> 00:21:56.520
Yeah, exactly. But there's no teaching you via pop-up commands or anything like that what you need to do.

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:59.720
He just lets you punch him no matter what.

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:03.160
Like you must punch him first before he ever starts doing anything to you.

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:05.140
That's true. And I think that's a smart thing to do.

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:08.940
It's like if you want to connect, if you want to learn how to play this game, you've got to connect to punch.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:12.320
Mm-hmm. I didn't, yeah, I guess I did notice that.

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:20.700
I thought it was just an AI thing where you had to make a move and he would just like try to counter it at first.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:23.340
But no, it makes sense that, yeah, you have to land a blow.

00:22:24.080 --> 00:22:26.560
Yeah. Any other thoughts on Glass Joe at all?

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:28.740
I mean, he's funny, I think.

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:30.240
What a poor name.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:35.540
Imagine if you're being introduced into your boxing, not your debut.

00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:36.900
Like you've been around for a while.

00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:39.640
A long, long time. Oh, I mean, there's Glass.

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:41.420
It's like a WWE character.

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:43.360
Yeah, there's Glass Mike.

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:45.860
I wonder how he's going to do today.

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:47.020
Aside from Mr.

00:22:47.140 --> 00:22:56.800
Dream himself, I think he is the closest thing to another icon in the mythos of Punch-Out.

00:22:56.800 --> 00:23:00.960
Like Glass Joe is probably the most famous character outside of Little Mac.

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:03.580
Because anybody who's ever played Punch-Out is playing against Glass Joe.

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:04.980
He always goes first.

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:06.660
I believe he's in Super Punch-Out, right?

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:07.740
He is. He's the first person you play against.

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:09.000
And he was in Punch-Out the Arcade.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:10.100
He's the first person you play against.

00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:12.060
Poor guy's getting beat up for his whole life.

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:16.640
Yeah. And it's kind of those in-between rounds things, which we haven't really talked about yet.

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:28.720
But in-between rounds, you have the corner and you hear Doc Lewis talking to you and the other enemy kind of giving a hint to how to fight them.

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:30.320
And I like that.

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:32.800
But with Glass Joe, there's no hint.

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:37.060
It's just like, please, let's just get this over with.

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:40.860
Can I take a nap before the next round, I think is one of his lines.

00:23:41.500 --> 00:23:43.020
So poor Glass Joe.

00:23:43.420 --> 00:23:44.520
Thankfully, he's pretty easy.

00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:47.480
So I don't mean that it's not a tutorial in a bad way.

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:55.160
I mean, it's nice that they didn't feel the need to explain it to you but made it easy enough that you can just kind of learn the ropes right there for the entirety of the game.

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:57.960
Every mechanic that you need is presented in there.

00:23:58.260 --> 00:24:08.140
Yeah. And one thing we haven't talked about yet is the accumulation of stars.

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:10.460
Does anybody want to try to tackle that?

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:11.900
So I got this.

00:24:11.900 --> 00:24:26.240
So the way that stars work are when you press start, you get a – and when you have a star, you press start to do a special uppercut that does a ton of damage and it's good for knocking people out.

00:24:26.460 --> 00:24:32.640
And the way you get those stars is by hitting people in certain ways.

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:39.520
I think that one of them is if you get enough uppercuts against – I forget which person.

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:41.080
It's unique to each character.

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:44.100
Yeah. And this is something Sean and I were talking about and it's hard to figure out.

00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:45.460
You know, you really need to look it up.

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:47.960
But it's also something you just experience on your own too.

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:52.200
And later in the game, they become essential and you actually need them to beat the characters.

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:55.960
Yeah. And I think that the uppercut inclusion is nice.

00:24:56.100 --> 00:25:06.380
It's nice that it's mapped to the start button, a button that you can't otherwise do anything with because much to probably Joe's disappointment, there's no pause sound in this game because you cannot pause.

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:09.120
You know, I don't think I tried to pause the game.

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:12.260
Wow. It doesn't seem like you should pause, but I'm having a great time.

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:13.360
I'm having a great time in the middle of a boxing match.

00:25:14.080 --> 00:25:17.940
You should think that, like, I'm going to be the one that's going to try and pause every game.

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:21.700
So I apologize. This is how Winter Games should have handled pausing.

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:22.800
Do you remember Winter Games?

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:24.980
The whole down start.

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:29.140
And then you, you know, like if you let go of it, it just resumed.

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:30.940
It should have just been no pausing at all.

00:25:31.300 --> 00:25:33.980
You can't pause in the middle of your events and your sport.

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:35.180
Really, all games.

00:25:35.380 --> 00:25:36.460
Don't make sense. All games should.

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:38.560
Yeah. It doesn't make sense. I just had a random thought with Winter Games.

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:40.820
What if you hold it and then unplug the controller?

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:42.760
Do you think that would work? Wow, we should try that.

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:43.960
Yeah. Okay, I'll try that.

00:25:44.060 --> 00:25:45.460
No, I'm serious. I'll try that. That's a great thought.

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:49.280
I'm sure it won't work. I'm sure it will just assume that you've let go of Stark.

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:51.360
This podcast is over.

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:55.020
See us next week after we've tried the removing of the controller.

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:59.840
Please switch to side B on the whole episode of what happens when you remove the controller.

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:01.060
So that's the stars.

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:02.340
And then there's hearts, too.

00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:03.900
Anybody want to try and give hearts a go?

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:07.760
Hearts is basically your stamina.

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:12.480
Yeah. Every time you miss, you'll lose hearts.

00:26:12.660 --> 00:26:14.200
Every time you get hit, you'll lose hearts.

00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:17.780
It's the well from which you pull your energy to do anything.

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:20.160
And once you're out of hearts, you turn pink, right?

00:26:20.240 --> 00:26:21.140
You're like glow pink.

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:22.160
You are very tired.

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:23.640
And you're tired and you cannot attack.

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:24.460
You can only dodge.

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:29.760
Yeah. And you have to dodge for essentially five seconds before you can start punching again.

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:36.520
I think it's a great mechanic, though, because it also allows for rallies to happen.

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:49.720
Like you see the guy getting hit in the boxing match in real life, and he can either just go down or he'll weather the storm for a while and come back with a storm of his own.

00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:53.860
It's a great punishment, too, to not just keep trying body check after body check.

00:26:54.020 --> 00:26:55.500
It forces you to be decisive.

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:58.140
Even the blocks count against your stamina.

00:26:58.300 --> 00:27:09.080
If you keep punching him in the stomach and Glass Joe keeps blocking it, you're actually losing stamina by doing that because you would get winded in a real boxing match if you just kept throwing your right arm again and again.

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:12.240
So the one system accomplishes multiple things.

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:14.260
That's great. And did anybody else?

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:17.540
I kind of skipped over this real fast, but the intermission between rounds.

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:19.280
Did anybody have any other thoughts on that?

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:23.620
I think it's a pretty cool way to handle the only time you can pause.

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:25.460
I like how it changes depending on how you're doing.

00:27:25.680 --> 00:27:29.180
Yeah. If you get beat up a lot, then Little Mac looks like shit.

00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:31.280
His eye is swollen.

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:33.400
He's got a little purple around it, too.

00:27:33.640 --> 00:27:34.780
He's saying the opposition, too.

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:36.820
And he says, I don't know how much longer I can take it.

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:41.660
Yeah. And then the opposition, you see how they're doing as well.

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:45.300
And sometimes they get angry and sometimes they're smug.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:47.040
So I think it's really cool.

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:52.760
One thing I talked to Sam about, and I read this, so I don't know how much it's confirmed,

00:27:52.860 --> 00:28:00.760
but if you press select as soon as the round goes to the cut scene, when you resume the next fight,

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:04.840
you get a ton more energy or health, essentially.

00:28:05.220 --> 00:28:10.340
You get a ton more health back because that's kind of like helping apply the bandages.

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:12.060
That is in the manual.

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:14.520
Oh, okay. Great. So it is an actual thing.

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:20.700
Yeah. I think a lot of this game already, we're only on Glass Joe, but it's showing a lot of promise

00:28:20.700 --> 00:28:23.580
because of all the little details that it puts into it.

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:29.840
So after Glass Joe, you face Von Kaiser, and I don't really have much to say about him

00:28:29.840 --> 00:28:35.180
other than he was the one where I learned my essential strategy for most of the enemies in the game,

00:28:35.180 --> 00:28:40.440
which is after you knock down Von Kaiser the first time, he will most likely get up.

00:28:40.700 --> 00:28:43.420
It'll be one, two, three, and then he'll get back up.

00:28:43.660 --> 00:28:50.060
And then if you dodge his attack, whatever comes next, dodge it, hit him with an uppercut,

00:28:50.180 --> 00:28:54.240
you can switch between uppercut left, uppercut right, uppercut left, uppercut right.

00:28:54.240 --> 00:28:56.080
You mean like a hook left or right?

00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:57.300
Oh, sorry. Yeah. Hook left, hook right.

00:28:57.300 --> 00:29:03.860
Yeah, like that. And you can just continue to chain it again and again as long as you alternate between left, right, left, right.

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:05.680
He'll never break out of it.

00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:10.020
Now, again, he must be knocked down once before this happens, but he'll never break out of it

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:11.680
and he'll go right to being knocked down again.

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:15.180
It's not much fun, but if I'm playing through this game from the beginning again

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:18.060
and I want to get through it pretty fast, you better believe I'm going to use that strategy.

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:20.540
It also feels pretty awesome the first time you do it.

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:21.860
Yeah, so it gets me pumped.

00:29:22.020 --> 00:29:24.740
Yeah. So that's Von Kaiser.

00:29:24.760 --> 00:29:25.700
Any other thoughts on him?

00:29:26.360 --> 00:29:31.560
Okay. And then Piston Honda, a guy we've been talking about a lot because he is the first like massive

00:29:32.060 --> 00:29:33.100
character you fight.

00:29:33.700 --> 00:29:36.060
Kind of looks like the guy from Street Fighter.

00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:37.740
Which guy from Street Fighter?

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:39.420
Oh, Ryu?

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:43.100
No. Maybe I'm thinking of a different fighting game.

00:29:43.100 --> 00:29:44.840
I think you are thinking of a different fighting game.

00:29:45.460 --> 00:29:47.920
Okay. Yeah. I'll just go back into my corner.

00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:50.500
I was like, yeah, because I don't think he really looks like Ryu, but I was trying to

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:52.540
throw you a bone. Sam?

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:57.260
This was the first one that when I was playing this the first time, it kind of felt like

00:29:57.260 --> 00:30:00.740
a real fight. Where the other ones, you can kind of cheese your way out of it.

00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:07.760
This one, really, it's, I think this is where the game actually starts and stops being a tutorial

00:30:07.760 --> 00:30:11.760
and starts being more of the game because he's got his little special fight that he does.

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:13.940
Yeah, the back up and then approach.

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:17.040
He backs up and then he charges and he has the Fists of Fury or whatever it is.

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:20.160
So what was it, when that first happened to you guys, because I think that's the first

00:30:20.160 --> 00:30:21.900
like trick move in the game, right?

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:24.900
Yeah. When that came out, what was your first strategy?

00:30:25.080 --> 00:30:28.640
Don't tell me what you actually have to do, but what was your first reaction to that?

00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:29.940
Did you just take the blows?

00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:32.060
Did you try to dodge to the left and right?

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:33.260
I think I tried to dodge.

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:35.060
Yeah, I tried to dodge too. I didn't do it very well.

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:40.560
Yeah. It was tough because, I mean, that was, especially the first time I saw that coming, I had no idea that

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:43.380
was coming, so it just kind of took me by surprise.

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:48.780
I think I tried to do a dodge punch like I tried with the other moves and that worked.

00:30:49.180 --> 00:30:54.220
Yeah. I wonder, has anybody been able to dodge left or right and successfully time it?

00:30:54.280 --> 00:30:57.760
Or will you be, by dodging to the left or right, will you eventually get hit by one of

00:30:57.760 --> 00:30:59.840
his punches? Like, can you perfectly time those?

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:03.060
Because I know if you hit down on the deep head, you'll walk.

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:08.260
Yeah, I think that you can get into a good enough rhythm so that you can be missed by all

00:31:08.260 --> 00:31:11.900
of them. You can also, if you hit down twice, I think you dodge as well.

00:31:11.900 --> 00:31:13.920
Oh, really? Okay. I did not notice that either.

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:15.400
I really need to check out this manual.

00:31:15.680 --> 00:31:16.860
I think that's true.

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:20.360
Okay. But I never dodged it successfully.

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:24.240
All that said, though, for Piston Honda, he's still an approachable character.

00:31:24.420 --> 00:31:26.460
The game hasn't hit, like, hard mode yet.

00:31:26.480 --> 00:31:29.080
It's just giving you something new that you have to prepare for.

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:32.800
But I think it was, like Sam said, I think it was the first time for me where I really

00:31:32.800 --> 00:31:37.200
had to think of a strategy or kind of decide, be more decisive with my attacks.

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:42.080
Like, when he does some crazy uppercut and that's when you got to jump in and, you know,

00:31:43.160 --> 00:31:44.420
throw all your attacks at once.

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:47.340
Right. And we mentioned a little, the quick way to get it.

00:31:47.340 --> 00:31:48.540
Well-timed body punch.

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:52.220
Yeah. And kudos to Sam for showing that to me.

00:31:52.380 --> 00:31:55.240
So then you become the champion of the minor circuit.

00:31:55.240 --> 00:31:57.320
There's a nice little screen for that and everything.

00:31:57.520 --> 00:31:59.820
And then there's the amazing cut scene that we just talked about.

00:32:00.020 --> 00:32:01.900
Where he's in Staten Island?

00:32:02.060 --> 00:32:03.140
Staten Island. Let's go with it.

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:05.840
Maybe a lower, maybe West Village.

00:32:06.160 --> 00:32:11.320
And it was interesting, too, because I, really, my only knowledge of Little Mac was from my also

00:32:11.320 --> 00:32:12.840
limited knowledge of Super Smash Brothers.

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:15.880
And there's a skin in Super Smash Brothers where he's wearing a pink hoodie.

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:19.100
And I always thought it was just a funny thing they put in there, but it's from that cut scene.

00:32:19.320 --> 00:32:22.160
Well, then you'll also know in Smash Brothers there's a wireframe version, too.

00:32:22.240 --> 00:32:24.460
Yeah, right. And that's from the arcade version.

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:27.840
You just pointed that out to me, just so you know.

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:32.380
I did not put the connection between the pink hoodie and the Smash Brothers skin.

00:32:32.640 --> 00:32:33.640
So that's really cool.

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:10.560
After the amazing cut scene, you go to the major circuit, which obviously would be a little step up from the minor circuit,

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:12.340
but you're not quite up to Mr.

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:13.540
Dream scale yet.

00:33:13.680 --> 00:33:18.880
And that's when you get, in my opinion, the real personality of the game starts to come out.

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:23.340
The first three characters definitely feel like people you might run into in a boxing match.

00:33:23.540 --> 00:33:25.540
You know? They're real people.

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:27.920
Then Don Flamenco comes out.

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:30.160
And that music, you know?

00:33:30.280 --> 00:33:32.880
Which I imagine is just royalty-free music, right?

00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:35.080
What is that? I forget what song it is.

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:38.880
Yeah. Yeah, anyway.

00:33:39.040 --> 00:33:40.460
He comes out to that with a rose.

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:42.980
Smash-sounding, yeah. It's a famous song.

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:45.600
I can't remember. Yeah, it's just so funny that he's doing this.

00:33:46.220 --> 00:33:50.220
And that kind of put a smile on my face coming off of the cut scene.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:51.720
You know? I'm already like, let's do it.

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:53.100
Next round. And then you see this.

00:33:53.100 --> 00:33:54.380
And I'm like, I can fight this guy.

00:33:55.520 --> 00:33:56.780
He's better than Glass Joe.

00:33:57.200 --> 00:33:58.260
Kind of looks like Glass Joe.

00:33:58.700 --> 00:33:59.680
A little? A little bit.

00:33:59.820 --> 00:34:00.880
Okay. He's tannin.

00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:03.620
We're having a hard time visualizing things right now.

00:34:03.900 --> 00:34:06.400
Don Flamenco had black hair.

00:34:06.600 --> 00:34:08.880
I was seeing Honda from Street Fighter.

00:34:12.100 --> 00:34:15.920
I think a big part of it is just that he's, since Glass Joe, we've seen a lot of people

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:16.980
that are like twice your size.

00:34:17.080 --> 00:34:18.200
This is another guy that's like more your size.

00:34:18.220 --> 00:34:19.620
So it reminds me. He's a skinny guy.

00:34:19.820 --> 00:34:21.920
Probably not 107 pounds like yourself.

00:34:22.220 --> 00:34:25.020
But nevertheless, he's a pro 17-year-old.

00:34:25.020 --> 00:34:29.540
But yeah, Flamenco sort of gets by just by taunting you the whole time.

00:34:29.780 --> 00:34:31.780
Yeah. So that's new.

00:34:32.180 --> 00:34:33.640
I like that though too.

00:34:33.640 --> 00:34:38.520
The idea of a cocky fighter who thinks like, oh, you're from the minor circuit.

00:34:38.660 --> 00:34:39.500
I'm on the major circuit.

00:34:39.540 --> 00:34:41.720
Your cuffs are 17 and 107 pounds.

00:34:42.560 --> 00:34:44.780
Right. This is probably going to be pretty easy.

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:47.500
I solved Flamenco with the old left and right again.

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:49.500
Just doing the knockdown.

00:34:49.660 --> 00:34:50.940
And then when it gets back up doing that.

00:34:51.360 --> 00:34:55.100
But there are people who play this game that get very creative with knocking people out

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:56.260
as fast as possible.

00:34:56.800 --> 00:35:01.560
And one thing that's really interesting with the speed running scene is that they don't

00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:03.900
time the game from real time duration.

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:06.280
They just go from fight to fight.

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:09.860
And then at the end of each fight, they add up the total time because it tells you like,

00:35:09.940 --> 00:35:15.300
you beat him in two minutes, point, two minutes, 13 seconds, point two, you know.

00:35:15.420 --> 00:35:18.420
Interesting. And they add up each of those to get a final thing.

00:35:18.700 --> 00:35:23.320
Is it just because like the time between rounds is set?

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:26.560
I guess it's just more like, oh, how fast can you hit the start button?

00:35:26.860 --> 00:35:29.920
You know, like making sure that, you know, there's no skill really involved there.

00:35:29.920 --> 00:35:32.640
So I guess there was more just making sure that the fights count.

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:37.480
Yeah. But it's people figuring out, you know, and this is not specifically for Don Flamenco,

00:35:37.640 --> 00:35:41.720
but there's people who figured out, oh, if you knock this guy out at exactly 43 seconds,

00:35:41.720 --> 00:35:43.560
it will just count as a KO.

00:35:44.020 --> 00:35:47.380
Like he will not get up as long as you do the timer at 43 seconds.

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:51.760
Or if you give this guy two star punches in a row, he'll TKO.

00:35:52.060 --> 00:35:55.520
You know, it's little things like that that people learn throughout it that I think is

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:57.580
really interesting. Me, I'm just going at them.

00:35:57.740 --> 00:36:01.080
That 43 second thing seems like RNG though.

00:36:01.380 --> 00:36:02.740
Not RNG because it's not random.

00:36:02.900 --> 00:36:06.380
It's 43 seconds. But what logic is behind that?

00:36:06.540 --> 00:36:07.500
Yeah. I don't know. It's interesting.

00:36:07.720 --> 00:36:11.140
And who knows for all, I think the 43 is the only thing that's required.

00:36:11.140 --> 00:36:13.300
I don't think it's a specific series of inputs.

00:36:13.640 --> 00:36:17.820
So yeah, it seems like almost it was coded into the game to have that.

00:36:18.220 --> 00:36:20.760
Interesting. Any other thoughts on Flamenco?

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:22.980
Who's not Flamenco?

00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:29.080
After that, we have King Hippo, who I would argue is the first trick fight of the game

00:36:29.080 --> 00:36:30.080
and maybe the only one.

00:36:30.700 --> 00:36:31.960
What do you mean by trick fight?

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:37.640
So you can't actually do anything to him unless you, like he must be KO'd.

00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:41.900
Yeah. And the only way to attack him is to knock down his pants.

00:36:43.020 --> 00:36:46.140
Oh yes. Over and over again.

00:36:46.160 --> 00:36:47.040
Like you can't, yeah exactly.

00:36:47.260 --> 00:36:53.440
You can't hurt him through anything other than him winding up for his punch, which is different

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:56.740
because he winds up for his punch sometimes and you can't do anything to him.

00:36:56.740 --> 00:37:01.660
But if he winds up his punch and opens his mouth, that's your chance to punch him in the belly,

00:37:02.120 --> 00:37:05.700
knock down his shorts, and then punch him in the belly as many times as possible.

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:06.760
Everyone knows how shorts work.

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:09.340
Your hands up, your mouth's open, your shorts are going to fall down.

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:13.580
This fight was, I couldn't get past it for the longest time.

00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:19.200
Interesting. I had to look it up, but I didn't understand that I could only hit him if he raised his

00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:22.620
arm over his head with the mouth and stuff like that.

00:37:22.760 --> 00:37:23.840
Yeah. It was.

00:37:23.980 --> 00:37:25.940
He glows a little too on it though too, right?

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:27.580
He does like a brief shimmer.

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:34.200
Maybe. Yeah. I know that in the cut scene though, in between rounds, he talks about his mouth or Doc Lewis

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:35.160
talks about his mouth.

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:36.100
I forget who does it.

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:40.160
Well, first Little Mac says something, this is all paraphrasing, but Little Mac says something

00:37:40.160 --> 00:37:42.320
along the lines of like, I just can't get through to him.

00:37:42.600 --> 00:37:49.500
And Doc gives like a borderline hint, but then Hippo is the one who talks about his mouth.

00:37:49.700 --> 00:37:52.520
Yeah. And so that's like the key to like look for the mouth to be open.

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:57.840
And it's also just, you have to be, I was so focused just staring at his face like, is

00:37:57.840 --> 00:37:58.920
he going to tell?

00:37:59.040 --> 00:38:06.060
Is he going to tell? And that visual information making its way to my hands of like whether I should punch or

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:08.120
not, just wasn't getting through.

00:38:08.500 --> 00:38:10.840
Well, he's also got the bandage around his belly button.

00:38:11.460 --> 00:38:12.360
Oh, I didn't. Yeah.

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:14.520
I mean like that's there. I just, I didn't think about that.

00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:15.860
So we're hitting him in his weak spot.

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:17.720
Right. Yeah. That's, that's cool.

00:38:18.420 --> 00:38:19.380
I like King Hippo.

00:38:20.160 --> 00:38:22.080
I like him too. I think he's a great character.

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:27.720
I think this is the first one that actually like took some time to figure out the pattern.

00:38:28.280 --> 00:38:30.920
It's, it's a little more difficult to solve the pattern.

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:32.900
Yeah. The first time I faced him, I lost.

00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:35.940
Yeah. I think he's, I had no idea what to do.

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:38.480
One of the most iconic people in this whole game.

00:38:38.680 --> 00:38:42.640
Yeah. And he shows up in all the games too, I think, including the, the Wii version that we'll

00:38:42.640 --> 00:38:45.280
talk about later. So that's King Hippo.

00:38:45.460 --> 00:38:49.640
He also shows up on the Captain N TV show, which we're not about to talk about today.

00:38:49.880 --> 00:38:51.780
Are you sure you don't want to go on a 15 minute?

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:57.160
Yeah. And join back to our Donkey Kong days when we were talking about the Donkey Kong TV show,

00:38:57.340 --> 00:38:58.160
which was a thing.

00:38:58.340 --> 00:38:59.820
People check it out on YouTube.

00:39:00.160 --> 00:39:02.040
It even got a Donkey Kong Junior show.

00:39:02.260 --> 00:39:03.400
Let's keep talking about Donkey Kong.

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:05.900
Let's do this. Yeah. Why isn't Donkey Kong in this game?

00:39:06.280 --> 00:39:07.260
Wouldn't that be amazing?

00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:09.720
Isn't he? Isn't, oh no.

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:11.420
You're thinking of in the Punch-Out!

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:12.780
Arcade version, he's in the crowd.

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:14.180
Oh, okay. Yeah.

00:39:14.380 --> 00:39:17.980
It's him, Mario, and somebody else.

00:39:18.360 --> 00:39:19.680
Cool. Probably Luigi. Cool.

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:21.640
And then in Super Punch-Out! I think even Yoshi's there.

00:39:22.040 --> 00:39:25.140
Aw. Which is weird that they let a dinosaur into the, uh, Luigi.

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:27.080
He's an adorable dinosaur.

00:39:27.260 --> 00:39:31.500
Yes, he is. Uh, speaking of adorable, we have Great Tiger up next.

00:39:31.860 --> 00:39:39.280
Not that adorable. Great Tiger is not that adorable, but he's pretty crazy because he has that, uh, onslaught

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:44.240
of attacks that he does with his special, where if you thought Piston Honda was bad, he just

00:39:44.240 --> 00:39:45.920
keeps coming at you and at you.

00:39:46.140 --> 00:39:51.560
And this is the one for me where every time he came at me, I was very low on stamina and

00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:57.260
usually wound up, at the very least, if I wasn't knocked down by him, I was, uh, getting

00:39:57.260 --> 00:40:01.160
those pink flashes because I was, I wasn't able to punch back because I was doing too much

00:40:01.160 --> 00:40:02.080
blocking. Mm-hmm.

00:40:02.240 --> 00:40:06.140
Right. Well, this one took, it took me a while to get through, not a while, but it was difficult

00:40:06.140 --> 00:40:07.420
for me to get through the first round.

00:40:07.880 --> 00:40:11.460
Um, but then in the second round, that's when he starts, like, zipping around the ring.

00:40:11.600 --> 00:40:13.220
Yeah. And I was like, what's going on?

00:40:13.320 --> 00:40:14.340
He's teleporting.

00:40:14.500 --> 00:40:17.260
I was so panicked when all of a sudden he was just, like, zooming around.

00:40:17.700 --> 00:40:21.700
It was the, that was the first thing that really, um, the first opponent that was really

00:40:22.900 --> 00:40:27.380
shockingly. But he's got the glowing, uh, head thing.

00:40:27.580 --> 00:40:30.320
On his turban, he has a, on his turban, he has a jewel.

00:40:30.620 --> 00:40:31.660
Yeah. And I liked that.

00:40:31.700 --> 00:40:32.860
I thought that was neat. Mm-hmm.

00:40:33.140 --> 00:40:34.200
It's like, okay, sure.

00:40:34.900 --> 00:40:38.520
And, you know, like, it's better than the eyes glowing or the punch or something like

00:40:38.520 --> 00:40:39.720
that. So I'm down with it.

00:40:40.360 --> 00:40:43.880
Um, another thing I wanted to say about Great Tiger, though, besides the on-slave attacks,

00:40:44.060 --> 00:40:49.040
is that he does hint at the teleporting thing at the very beginning of the match when he gets

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:55.400
out of the corner. He does, like, ever so slightly, yeah, like, teleport just a little bit further into the

00:40:55.400 --> 00:40:57.640
stage, which could look like a glitch, you know?

00:40:57.800 --> 00:41:01.880
Oh, yeah. If you don't know the special move yet, it could just look like, oh, that was weird.

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:04.060
Like, he just moved enough.

00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:07.720
Like, it wasn't, it wasn't like he's teleporting around the entire ring.

00:41:08.200 --> 00:41:10.860
Yeah. It was like, get ready for this thing that's coming up.

00:41:11.180 --> 00:41:15.780
Right. I actually really liked that special move, though, despite how it requires, like, very

00:41:15.780 --> 00:41:20.940
precise timing of blocks or maybe a correct attack at the right time.

00:41:20.940 --> 00:41:24.880
I really thought that was interesting because now it's, it's like we're in a different world.

00:41:25.240 --> 00:41:28.980
You know? Everybody so far has been grounded to reality in one way or another.

00:41:29.180 --> 00:41:33.140
Yeah. And then Great Tiger comes and he's like, oh, by the way, I can teleport like Goku.

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:37.180
Aside from maybe King Hippo not looking like a human being.

00:41:37.180 --> 00:41:38.660
But I get what you're saying.

00:41:38.660 --> 00:41:41.440
I'm sure somebody looks like that and they're offended by you saying that.

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:44.360
I didn't, I don't know, I didn't notice it.

00:41:44.600 --> 00:41:49.480
I knew afterwards from watching Let's Plays and stuff, but I didn't notice it as teleporting.

00:41:49.520 --> 00:41:53.080
I thought it was just their way of showing him, like, running around, being really fast.

00:41:53.760 --> 00:41:55.380
But, um, but I guess it's supposed to be teleportation.

00:41:55.380 --> 00:41:56.320
That's really fast, Joe. Yeah.

00:41:56.480 --> 00:41:57.680
That was, you know, that was a thing.

00:41:57.760 --> 00:41:59.080
He's just zipping around. He's a vampire.

00:41:59.080 --> 00:42:00.300
I mean, isn't that what Goku does?

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:02.720
He just runs really fast and it looks like he's teleporting?

00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:04.240
No, it's called instant transmission.

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:05.280
Oh, I don't see it.

00:42:05.340 --> 00:42:06.180
That shows what I know.

00:42:06.180 --> 00:42:08.740
He puts his two fingers on his forehead and it just happens.

00:42:08.780 --> 00:42:10.080
It goes, zink. Yeah.

00:42:10.580 --> 00:42:11.520
That's such a cool sound.

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:12.540
Something like that, yeah. Yeah.

00:42:13.300 --> 00:42:14.460
And here's that sound right here.

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:15.920
No, no, no.

00:42:16.720 --> 00:42:18.700
Yeah, this is now a Dragon Ball Z show.

00:42:20.060 --> 00:42:22.080
What do you guys think of being Droid 18?

00:42:22.380 --> 00:42:26.780
So, Bald Bull is the next character on our list.

00:42:26.780 --> 00:42:28.860
Does anybody want to talk about Bald Bull?

00:42:29.840 --> 00:42:31.040
No. Bald Bull.

00:42:33.560 --> 00:42:35.460
Bald Bull would be interesting, though.

00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:37.240
Bald Bull is...

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:43.500
If there's one thing I'm going to say about this guy, it's that he's the equivalent of

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:46.420
Piston Honda in terms of someone who actually looks like a boxer.

00:42:46.680 --> 00:42:47.980
Yes. Yeah, he's scary looking.

00:42:48.200 --> 00:42:52.680
Yeah. He definitely doesn't...

00:42:53.300 --> 00:42:58.600
And... I guess I don't know what I want to say here, but he's not as challenging to me as Great

00:42:58.600 --> 00:43:01.080
Tiger was, so it's weird that he comes afterwards.

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:02.480
A bit of a respite.

00:43:02.920 --> 00:43:04.000
Do you? Then?

00:43:04.200 --> 00:43:08.120
Like, oh, I'm recovering from this hard fight with a more easy fight?

00:43:08.240 --> 00:43:09.800
I don't think it's an easy fight, though, I guess.

00:43:09.960 --> 00:43:15.100
Because even though it's not as tricky as Great Tiger, I did find that Bald Bull's punches

00:43:15.100 --> 00:43:17.780
did an awful lot of damage to Little Mac.

00:43:18.040 --> 00:43:22.700
So I actually found myself getting knocked down a lot, and that's tough because Bald Bull can't

00:43:22.700 --> 00:43:24.060
be KO'd. Yes.

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:25.380
He can only...

00:43:25.380 --> 00:43:26.220
Can't be KO'd?

00:43:26.220 --> 00:43:27.860
He can't be... He can only get TKO'd.

00:43:28.360 --> 00:43:29.540
Oh, oh, oh. You can't...

00:43:29.540 --> 00:43:30.580
Knock him down three times, yeah.

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:32.400
Exactly. You have to knock him down three times.

00:43:32.620 --> 00:43:37.040
He's unbeatable by decision, so if you go all three rounds, you won't be able to...

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:39.580
They'll just decide that Bald Bull won the match.

00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:41.760
And I guess...

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:43.320
Bald Bull. We'll just call him BB going forward.

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:45.580
I think that's kind of BS, you know?

00:43:45.820 --> 00:43:50.060
Because if you're like a little kid, and you knock down this guy more than he knocks you

00:43:50.060 --> 00:43:51.100
down, I think you win.

00:43:51.580 --> 00:43:53.480
Yeah. I actually don't know.

00:43:53.640 --> 00:43:54.920
I've never won by decision.

00:43:55.200 --> 00:43:57.160
If I'm just going off a little bit here.

00:43:57.380 --> 00:44:01.600
Well, chances are if it takes you three rounds to get through a fight, you're probably not

00:44:01.600 --> 00:44:02.560
doing that well.

00:44:02.560 --> 00:44:04.920
But is it possible? Yeah, but boy, he's also not doing that well.

00:44:05.080 --> 00:44:07.020
You know? He's also fighting me.

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:08.300
Yeah, a 17-year-old boy.

00:44:08.300 --> 00:44:12.500
A 17-year-old 107-pound boy, and he can't beat me, so what does that say about him?

00:44:12.500 --> 00:44:14.100
Yeah, if you go three rounds with Mr.

00:44:14.240 --> 00:44:16.540
Dream, I feel like you should just be decided the

00:44:16.540 --> 00:44:19.620
winner. Yeah. But, so, Joe, did you win a round?

00:44:19.620 --> 00:44:21.860
So, I did not win by decision, but I...

00:44:22.300 --> 00:44:26.540
Afterwards, after I stopped and couldn't get any farther, when I was watching a Let's Play,

00:44:26.720 --> 00:44:28.840
I saw actually against Mr.

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:30.400
Dream. Interesting. They won by decision.

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:31.380
Really? Yeah. Okay.

00:44:31.640 --> 00:44:32.920
I thought it was... Oh, wow.

00:44:32.980 --> 00:44:34.420
I did not know that that was possible.

00:44:35.200 --> 00:44:36.700
So, with Bald Bull, I...

00:44:36.700 --> 00:44:42.520
Yeah. Uh, there's another one of those, uh, secrets, secret ways of, uh, of ending him.

00:44:42.720 --> 00:44:47.480
Okay. Which is, uh, when he does his little bull hop thing...

00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:49.020
Gotta hit him in the stomach again.

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:51.100
Yeah, you hit him in the stomach on the third hop.

00:44:51.360 --> 00:44:54.760
He jumps towards you three times, and I think...

00:44:54.760 --> 00:44:59.580
And then he attacks you, and on the third hop, the second he jumps, or while he's...

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:01.200
I'm not exactly sure what the timing is.

00:45:01.780 --> 00:45:04.420
If you do a body blow, then it just knocks him right out.

00:45:04.420 --> 00:45:08.900
Yeah, and I think you kind of, like, you have to do something like that, because he goes

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:10.340
into, like, an infinite loop...

00:45:10.340 --> 00:45:11.700
Yeah. ...of that, you know?

00:45:11.800 --> 00:45:12.660
So, if you're...

00:45:12.660 --> 00:45:16.920
If you don't know what to do, you're just gonna have to continue blocking that infinite

00:45:16.920 --> 00:45:22.240
loop of him doing this weird leapfrog game, where he, like, backs up into the corner,

00:45:22.440 --> 00:45:23.580
and then boom, boom, boom.

00:45:23.960 --> 00:45:24.860
That's what I'm talking about.

00:45:24.860 --> 00:45:30.240
I think it's with 40 seconds left that always triggers, because I was trying over and over

00:45:30.240 --> 00:45:33.600
and over just to knock him down three times before that would happen.

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:38.980
Yeah. But I think it also will jump into that if you knock him down twice, so that you can't

00:45:38.980 --> 00:45:39.880
just TKO...

00:45:39.880 --> 00:45:45.440
You can't TKO him before he does those charges.

00:45:45.700 --> 00:45:53.120
Yeah. And yeah.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:46:21.620
yeah. And now we will move on to the world circuit where we see the repeat of Piston Honda,

00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:27.240
Ball Bowl, and Don Flamenco.

00:46:27.400 --> 00:46:29.120
They return. They're a little bit harder.

00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:31.120
I don't want to talk about them again, though,

00:46:31.120 --> 00:46:37.340
but one thing I did want to call out that I watched a YouTube video on was that the second time you fight Piston Honda,

00:46:37.740 --> 00:46:40.880
there is, and it was known in the community for a long time,

00:46:41.060 --> 00:46:46.960
that there's a special timing for a punch that will knock out Piston Honda and end the match.

00:46:47.200 --> 00:46:51.740
But nobody could figure out how to, like, what was the timing or when to do it.

00:46:51.800 --> 00:46:59.060
But there actually is a visual cue in the game that was staring at gamers all across the globe

00:46:59.060 --> 00:47:03.640
for 20 years before it was finally discovered about a year or two ago.

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:08.120
In the audience, those pink characters with the black silhouettes, you know,

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:10.220
who I'm talking about, like all the little heads.

00:47:10.520 --> 00:47:16.120
In the left side, right behind where Piston Honda would be standing,

00:47:16.920 --> 00:47:21.120
as soon as the timing comes, the audience member ducks.

00:47:22.020 --> 00:47:23.320
They actually duck.

00:47:23.740 --> 00:47:27.940
And if you punch, if you give him an uppercut, as soon as that character ducks,

00:47:28.020 --> 00:47:32.660
which only happens in that round, you will knock him out and it will be a TKO.

00:47:32.660 --> 00:47:33.520
That's actually amazing.

00:47:33.700 --> 00:47:38.460
I want a spinoff game about that character and him trying to give you hints.

00:47:39.020 --> 00:47:40.020
Right? Like, what does he know?

00:47:40.180 --> 00:47:41.940
Isn't there a camera one also?

00:47:41.940 --> 00:47:46.780
I think there's a few video audience cues,

00:47:47.260 --> 00:47:51.300
but that one stood out the most interesting to me because it wasn't discovered for 20 years.

00:47:51.400 --> 00:47:55.960
There had to have been some kid in 1987 or a few of them that knew about it,

00:47:56.040 --> 00:47:58.400
like found out and just kept their mouth shut for so long.

00:47:59.200 --> 00:48:00.600
Yeah, I'm keeping this to myself.

00:48:01.880 --> 00:48:02.780
It's really cool, though.

00:48:02.940 --> 00:48:04.180
I mean, I never looked at the audience.

00:48:04.500 --> 00:48:07.220
I know there was some guy taking a picture, but I didn't think,

00:48:07.300 --> 00:48:09.680
oh, I should punch the guy while he's taking the picture.

00:48:10.560 --> 00:48:14.640
But we do face one new character, and that's Soda Popinski.

00:48:15.440 --> 00:48:16.400
Very Russian.

00:48:16.720 --> 00:48:18.680
Originally Vodka Drunkinski.

00:48:19.660 --> 00:48:23.700
More Russian. Yeah, that was censored by Nintendo of America.

00:48:24.120 --> 00:48:27.880
There were some unfortunate stereotypes going on at some point.

00:48:28.060 --> 00:48:31.440
And, yeah, he's very Russian, to Sean's point,

00:48:31.500 --> 00:48:34.700
so that's probably why they didn't want to do the Vodka Drunkinski.

00:48:35.400 --> 00:48:37.700
So, anything to say about Soda?

00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:41.480
Soda? I honestly don't think I made it to him.

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:45.260
I think I could only get through the repeats.

00:48:45.720 --> 00:48:47.120
Okay. Well, you don't fade it.

00:48:47.200 --> 00:48:48.420
I don't think the repeats, sorry.

00:48:48.540 --> 00:48:53.900
I don't think it's Piston Honda, Ball, Ball, Ball, and Don Flamenco before Soda, is it?

00:48:54.160 --> 00:48:57.100
I only played a couple guys I had already played before.

00:48:57.400 --> 00:49:04.420
I'm pretty sure, yeah. Anyway, Soda Popinski, the long story short on him is that it's just at this point in the game,

00:49:04.580 --> 00:49:07.220
all of the fights are very challenging, in my opinion.

00:49:07.740 --> 00:49:10.540
This is the kind of, once you get to the world circuit,

00:49:10.840 --> 00:49:15.480
we're at the point now where it's going to come down to a few times playing through each character,

00:49:15.480 --> 00:49:19.740
just to get the groove of how to fight them, what to dodge.

00:49:19.740 --> 00:49:24.540
And even when you get up to them, there's no guarantee that you're going to perform perfectly.

00:49:24.860 --> 00:49:29.040
Yeah. You know, it's sad to say, but I feel like the next three characters,

00:49:29.220 --> 00:49:31.080
Mr. Sandman, Super Macho Man, and Mr.

00:49:31.220 --> 00:49:34.600
Dream, for the average player who's not obsessed with Punch-Out,

00:49:34.700 --> 00:49:37.500
they really don't stand a chance against those three guys.

00:49:37.940 --> 00:49:40.280
Yeah. Well, I'm living proof of that.

00:49:40.740 --> 00:49:44.840
Definitely. Not definitely you, but also definitely you.

00:49:45.600 --> 00:49:50.380
Mr. Sandman was as far as I got to anything.

00:49:50.560 --> 00:49:51.560
Same with me. Yeah.

00:49:51.760 --> 00:49:55.340
And I did not last more than the first round with Mr.

00:49:55.420 --> 00:50:00.600
Sandman. Even just trying to dodge things, my timing was just very awful.

00:50:01.060 --> 00:50:05.900
I was constantly getting uppercuts hit on me, and he takes off a massive amount.

00:50:05.940 --> 00:50:08.000
He hit on you, too? Yeah.

00:50:08.000 --> 00:50:09.900
Yeah, he, Mr.

00:50:10.180 --> 00:50:12.940
Sandman not only brought me a dream, but he hit on me.

00:50:14.200 --> 00:50:16.940
Jeez. They really want to just embarrass you here.

00:50:17.000 --> 00:50:22.300
So I don't want to say, like, that the game isn't fair, because it is definitely fair.

00:50:22.300 --> 00:50:27.200
You know, you just, it requires a certain dexterity that I do not have.

00:50:27.460 --> 00:50:34.680
I do not have the capacity to time my dodges and my punches to match these guys.

00:50:34.820 --> 00:50:36.300
These guys are playing on...

00:50:36.760 --> 00:50:38.060
Four-dimensional chess here.

00:50:38.220 --> 00:50:40.220
Yeah, four-dimensional chess, but also steroids.

00:50:40.580 --> 00:50:44.500
I think it also just requires an amount of practice.

00:50:44.900 --> 00:50:48.160
Yeah. That's what I think is so cool about these games, is that it's, I think it's the first

00:50:48.160 --> 00:50:50.440
time we've seen boss battles like this.

00:50:50.600 --> 00:50:55.060
I mean, that are, that you really have to learn and practice and get that satisfaction of beating

00:50:55.060 --> 00:51:00.140
a boss afterwards. I mean, I think the closest thing was maybe Twin B had some things we had to learn what

00:51:00.140 --> 00:51:04.740
was happening, but this is the first, like, real, real boss that you have to, I mean,

00:51:04.820 --> 00:51:07.180
every, every battle, every fight, every battle.

00:51:07.480 --> 00:51:08.380
I'm not a boxer.

00:51:08.760 --> 00:51:10.700
You were talking about Stinger there, right?

00:51:12.040 --> 00:51:13.320
Twin B, we didn't...

00:51:13.320 --> 00:51:14.500
Twin B is the... Is the...

00:51:14.960 --> 00:51:16.220
Is the Japanese version? Is the Japanese version.

00:51:16.380 --> 00:51:18.780
Okay, yes. Yeah, yeah. Yeah, yeah.

00:51:18.840 --> 00:51:22.100
Stinger. Stinger, yep.

00:51:22.100 --> 00:51:27.120
You described this to me, though, earlier this week, that this game is essentially a

00:51:27.120 --> 00:51:27.980
boss rush. Right.

00:51:28.180 --> 00:51:32.420
Definitely. And I love that you said that, because I think that's the closest thing we've ever had to

00:51:32.420 --> 00:51:35.340
a boss rush in any NES game so far.

00:51:35.740 --> 00:51:36.920
Imagine if you had to... I just had the realization.

00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:42.880
Right. Imagine if you had to play, like, Legend of Zelda on Dungeon 8, right before Ganon, you

00:51:42.880 --> 00:51:47.140
have to fight every other villain again before you can get to Ganon.

00:51:47.280 --> 00:51:48.220
Like, that would be ridiculous.

00:51:48.780 --> 00:51:50.400
Here, that's the whole game.

00:51:50.660 --> 00:51:52.380
Right. Wow.

00:51:53.160 --> 00:51:56.960
And it feels to me a little more like modern boss battles.

00:51:57.500 --> 00:51:59.260
Just because of the way...

00:51:59.260 --> 00:52:01.840
On the NES, it's usually a little thing.

00:52:01.940 --> 00:52:04.280
Oh, you gotta hit it in that weak spot, and then you gotta figure that out.

00:52:04.340 --> 00:52:05.420
Or when this opens up, you hit it.

00:52:05.480 --> 00:52:09.920
But this is really pattern memory, or pattern recognition.

00:52:10.120 --> 00:52:14.480
When we were talking about the tells earlier, I wasn't sure if I was gonna be ridiculed for

00:52:14.480 --> 00:52:18.940
this, but I was looking at Joe, and Joe is a big Shadow of the Colossus fan, and played

00:52:18.940 --> 00:52:19.980
through that entire game.

00:52:19.980 --> 00:52:25.280
And now that we're talking about it, I need to ask, are there any similarities for you

00:52:25.280 --> 00:52:31.220
here between the Colossi in Shadow of the Colossus and how you deal with them, and then, like,

00:52:31.600 --> 00:52:35.040
these eight-bit interpretations of bosses here?

00:52:35.040 --> 00:52:38.100
I mean, now that you mention it, I hadn't thought of it that way, but now that you mention it,

00:52:38.160 --> 00:52:43.900
yeah. I mean, obviously, if you take away the obvious differences of Shadow of the Colossus being

00:52:43.900 --> 00:52:49.620
this giant, beautiful 3D rendered thing, but it's just the idea of recognizing, okay, what

00:52:49.620 --> 00:52:50.920
is this guy's quirk?

00:52:50.940 --> 00:52:56.600
Telegraphs. And what do you do? I actually thought a little bit of it, this is also, I don't want to be ridiculed for

00:52:56.600 --> 00:53:02.460
this either, but I was feeling the same feeling that I get when I'm fighting a boss in Kingdom

00:53:02.460 --> 00:53:03.460
Hearts. Mm-hmm.

00:53:03.600 --> 00:53:06.960
Because obviously those bosses are very different, you're running around, there's a lot more

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:11.680
strategy, but it's still, you're seeing, okay, so once I do this, he's gonna start

00:53:11.680 --> 00:53:14.120
doing this move now, and I gotta think of, like, how to counter that.

00:53:14.120 --> 00:53:21.520
I think any well-designed, like, 3D character action game that has the concept of bosses

00:53:22.280 --> 00:53:23.600
has this to some extent.

00:53:23.800 --> 00:53:27.140
Right, definitely, and I think that's why this is amazing to me, it's the first one that,

00:53:27.220 --> 00:53:32.460
it feels like what most 3D boss battles feel like, and it's just not in the NES.

00:53:32.620 --> 00:53:39.020
And you're not moving. I haven't played it, but this game reminded me of kind of what people talk about with

00:53:39.020 --> 00:53:42.400
Dark Souls, you know, with dodging and countering and everything.

00:53:42.680 --> 00:53:45.780
Okay. And I don't know if that's accurate, because I haven't played it.

00:53:45.900 --> 00:53:53.020
I think that, again, I think that Dark Souls, while it's more difficult than a lot of games,

00:53:53.220 --> 00:53:59.840
it still borrows heavily from every sort of action game that came before it, at least in

00:53:59.840 --> 00:54:07.040
how it designs bosses, except that every different enemy in that game is sort of designed like

00:54:07.040 --> 00:54:11.020
a boss, with tells and when you should attack and when you shouldn't.

00:54:11.020 --> 00:54:12.640
I like that we're having this discussion.

00:54:13.020 --> 00:54:19.140
I think it goes back to what Sean said, how every good boss fight should be feeling this

00:54:19.140 --> 00:54:22.960
way. And so I don't mean to say that when you get to Mr.

00:54:23.120 --> 00:54:24.760
Sandman, Super Macho Man, and Mr.

00:54:24.900 --> 00:54:26.580
Dream, that all of a sudden it's a bad game.

00:54:26.580 --> 00:54:31.680
But it is noticeably different that the other ones or the other fights all up until then

00:54:31.680 --> 00:54:34.020
were more approachable and maybe took some time.

00:54:34.160 --> 00:54:36.620
And I'm sure if I had more time, maybe I would learn Mr.

00:54:36.700 --> 00:54:39.760
Sandman. And then slowly more time I'd get through Super Macho Man.

00:54:39.840 --> 00:54:41.380
And who knows, maybe one day I'd beat Mr.

00:54:41.480 --> 00:54:47.780
Dream. I know that a lot of people all over the globe would talk about the NES and punch out specifically

00:54:47.780 --> 00:54:49.840
because it is considered one of the hardest NES games.

00:54:50.100 --> 00:54:53.860
There are a lot of people who also consider it a very fair game, similar to how we talk

00:54:53.860 --> 00:54:56.820
about Ghosts and Goblins, which not many people consider a fair game.

00:54:56.940 --> 00:55:00.780
But I never felt like it was punishing us in a way that we didn't deserve, other than maybe

00:55:00.780 --> 00:55:06.160
the Red Devil onslaught, which was maybe our first example of a boss rush, even though

00:55:06.160 --> 00:55:07.900
it's just a minor character in the game.

00:55:08.560 --> 00:55:12.220
But all that said, I kind of wish that Mr.

00:55:12.300 --> 00:55:13.620
Sandman, Super Macho Man, Mr.

00:55:13.780 --> 00:55:20.520
Dream, only ramped up slightly each time in difficulty instead of all of a sudden this floor that I couldn't

00:55:20.520 --> 00:55:25.680
overcome. Yeah, I think that difficulty curves are still a thing that everybody is working on right

00:55:25.680 --> 00:55:32.580
now, not only just for, again, prolonging the amount of time that it takes to finish a

00:55:32.580 --> 00:55:34.820
game. You're spending 60 bucks on the game.

00:55:34.820 --> 00:55:37.860
Yeah, but also just for game design in general.

00:55:38.100 --> 00:55:43.860
I do think, though, that because of the way the save system works, I mean, not that anyone

00:55:43.860 --> 00:55:47.240
said it wasn't fair, but I do think it's very fair in that it doesn't put you all the way

00:55:47.240 --> 00:55:48.200
back at the beginning. Yeah.

00:55:48.360 --> 00:55:50.420
You know, it sends you, what, to the beginning of the world circuit?

00:55:50.580 --> 00:55:51.480
The beginning of the circuit that you're on, yeah.

00:55:51.480 --> 00:55:53.960
Which is still a lot of people to get back to Mr.

00:55:54.080 --> 00:55:57.320
Sandman, but I think it's still...

00:55:57.320 --> 00:56:00.880
It's more fair than, say, going to have to start with Glass Joe again, absolutely.

00:56:01.180 --> 00:56:05.020
Yeah, yeah, yeah. So I think that's kind of, it's kind of cool because it doesn't set you back that much.

00:56:05.160 --> 00:56:11.140
And then by the end of it, you can breeze through, you know, Pissin' Honda, Bald Bull, and...

00:56:11.140 --> 00:56:16.280
Bald Bull. The rural juror.

00:56:16.380 --> 00:56:18.960
And Donnie Flamingo.

00:56:19.060 --> 00:56:20.400
Yeah. Donnie Flamingo.

00:56:20.460 --> 00:56:21.480
I like that. Yeah.

00:56:21.480 --> 00:56:26.140
So I would have liked the inclusion of a practice mode, maybe, where you could pick who you want

00:56:26.140 --> 00:56:29.780
to fight against just to learn the ropes, and it wouldn't count for anything, but you could

00:56:29.780 --> 00:56:31.240
learn how to play against the characters.

00:56:31.480 --> 00:56:35.440
But I could see how that also goes against the spirit of the game and getting the title.

00:56:35.640 --> 00:56:39.960
It's like, hey, Bald Bull, do you want to have a practice match in the back?

00:56:40.140 --> 00:56:41.460
Like, that wouldn't happen.

00:56:41.640 --> 00:56:42.580
Yeah. I don't think so.

00:56:42.760 --> 00:56:43.700
It goes against the lore.

00:56:43.700 --> 00:56:45.480
There is something interesting to point out.

00:56:45.580 --> 00:56:50.060
This is, we're now on a whole different level of talking about video games right now.

00:56:50.120 --> 00:56:57.380
But in SNK's 40th anniversary compilation, you can actually watch that game play itself,

00:56:57.380 --> 00:57:01.080
and then at any moment say, I'd like to take control.

00:57:01.500 --> 00:57:06.160
And like, so you can just watch the whole game play itself, get to the final battle and

00:57:06.160 --> 00:57:09.700
say, I'm ready to jump in and do that.

00:57:09.700 --> 00:57:13.840
And, you know, that kind of accessibility in this day and age is nice.

00:57:14.000 --> 00:57:19.560
I'm not saying it would have worked in 1987 to just watch a computer little Mac fight

00:57:19.560 --> 00:57:21.740
everybody and then be like, oh, cool, he's back up to Mr.

00:57:21.840 --> 00:57:23.000
Sandman. It's my turn now.

00:57:23.020 --> 00:57:30.240
I feel like stuff like that only happens when a game becomes so incredibly niche or has evolved

00:57:31.180 --> 00:57:33.980
inside of itself so much that fighting games have become.

00:57:34.140 --> 00:57:35.320
Think about what we're talking about, too.

00:57:35.420 --> 00:57:37.480
We're talking about a 40th anniversary compilation.

00:57:37.480 --> 00:57:39.860
Like, of course, it would have a feature like that.

00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:41.300
You know, it's a museum thing.

00:57:41.760 --> 00:57:43.860
It's not like, yeah, you know what I'm saying?

00:57:43.880 --> 00:57:47.580
It's not like you're about to go play, you know, Kingdom Hearts 3.

00:57:48.280 --> 00:57:49.360
He's going to let it play.

00:57:50.100 --> 00:57:51.480
Yeah, and just let it play.

00:57:51.900 --> 00:57:53.800
Like a whole, like, adventure game.

00:57:54.140 --> 00:57:57.380
Right. They're never going to put that in there because the whole point of the game was for you to

00:57:57.380 --> 00:58:01.000
play it. Yeah. So I'm just throwing that in hindsight.

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:04.540
I'm not saying, like, what were the developers thinking by not including that?

00:58:04.540 --> 00:58:07.620
I'm just saying, wow, wouldn't that be nice if that was here?

00:58:07.960 --> 00:58:12.020
This podcast is just now a collection of wouldn't that be nicest.

00:58:12.600 --> 00:58:16.580
Well, speaking of wouldn't that be nicest, then, kind of going back to what you were saying,

00:58:16.660 --> 00:58:21.220
Mike, about the difficulty curve kind of ramping up so quickly.

00:58:21.220 --> 00:58:28.740
I wonder how would you have felt if just before those last, what, four opponents, you beat

00:58:28.740 --> 00:58:30.460
the game. Like, that's the end of the game.

00:58:30.460 --> 00:58:32.720
And then these are, like, the bonus opponents.

00:58:32.940 --> 00:58:36.760
Would it feel more satisfying where it's like, all right, now I get the, like, end game, like,

00:58:36.820 --> 00:58:38.000
post-game opponents?

00:58:38.220 --> 00:58:39.040
I don't think so.

00:58:39.060 --> 00:58:41.220
Yeah, I agree. Because I was thinking of that.

00:58:41.220 --> 00:58:45.160
But I think that the difficulty curve, maybe it's a little steep all of a sudden.

00:58:45.260 --> 00:58:47.480
But I like that all of a sudden it gets really difficult.

00:58:47.480 --> 00:58:51.820
And that's where it's like, you have to train and you have to play this a lot to get past

00:58:51.820 --> 00:58:53.100
those. Oh, sorry.

00:58:53.420 --> 00:58:58.900
I'm thinking maybe a compromise could be a save point right before Soda Popinski.

00:58:59.400 --> 00:59:02.260
You know, after you already beat those three guys that you've beaten before.

00:59:02.660 --> 00:59:05.800
I think that could be, not that it's more fair.

00:59:06.700 --> 00:59:07.560
Yeah, I hear you.

00:59:07.700 --> 00:59:10.160
It just might be a little bit more, you know.

00:59:10.320 --> 00:59:11.860
It's just less annoying to have to replay.

00:59:12.120 --> 00:59:14.700
Yeah, it's interesting to consider the number of opponents.

00:59:14.700 --> 00:59:17.160
I mean, we had the minor circuit had three.

00:59:17.400 --> 00:59:20.700
The major circuit had, I think, four, maybe five.

00:59:21.180 --> 00:59:26.380
Four. And then the world circuit goes on to have one, two, three, four, five, six people in it.

00:59:26.500 --> 00:59:29.440
So it is a large jump all of a sudden.

00:59:29.800 --> 00:59:36.620
And then on top of that, to Joe's point, beating Super Macho Man is kind of like beating the game.

00:59:36.820 --> 00:59:37.640
Mr. Dream.

00:59:37.760 --> 00:59:39.600
It is treated as kind of like a bonus fight.

00:59:39.920 --> 00:59:41.860
You've already, like, won.

00:59:42.460 --> 00:59:44.660
He's not part of the world circuit.

00:59:44.960 --> 00:59:47.080
You beat Super Macho Man.

00:59:47.080 --> 00:59:49.060
You win. You get, like, this great cut scene.

00:59:49.180 --> 00:59:53.780
And then all of a sudden it treats it almost like a dream fight, you know, where all of a sudden it's—

00:59:53.780 --> 00:59:54.700
It's not really happening.

00:59:55.380 --> 00:59:56.420
That's what they call it, right?

00:59:56.500 --> 00:59:57.920
The dream fight? I think they do.

00:59:58.220 --> 01:00:00.220
And there's a weird thing going on.

01:00:00.300 --> 01:00:02.720
I don't know if it was because of the password that I played with.

01:00:02.720 --> 01:00:04.640
I typed in a password to get to Mr.

01:00:04.800 --> 01:00:05.640
Dream. Cheater.

01:00:05.780 --> 01:00:07.440
There's a very well-known password for it.

01:00:07.820 --> 01:00:12.280
And there's something about it that the stage and the ring just feel weird.

01:00:12.480 --> 01:00:13.440
They feel different.

01:00:14.160 --> 01:00:17.500
It's all shimmery. No, it kind of, like, blends in.

01:00:17.740 --> 01:00:18.740
You were there.

01:00:19.240 --> 01:00:20.840
The ring kind of, like, blends in.

01:00:20.900 --> 01:00:22.140
But that might have just been, like, my bad fight.

01:00:22.140 --> 01:00:23.380
Everyone's naked for some reason.

01:00:23.680 --> 01:00:26.160
No, I mean, that makes sense if it was a creative decision.

01:00:33.020 --> 01:00:34.920
So are we going to talk about the Mr.

01:00:35.060 --> 01:00:36.940
Dream fight? We need to talk about the Mr.

01:00:37.100 --> 01:00:38.280
Dream fight. No, that's it.

01:00:38.460 --> 01:00:42.400
Thanks, everyone. I thought that was the cue to start talking about it.

01:00:42.400 --> 01:00:43.760
Yes, please. Talk about the Mr.

01:00:43.920 --> 01:00:46.180
Dream fight. I don't know anything about the Mr.

01:00:46.560 --> 01:00:48.740
Dream fight. Nothing. So you need to do it.

01:00:48.860 --> 01:00:50.220
Okay, well— Can you guys do it?

01:00:50.440 --> 01:00:51.520
I didn't get to Mr.

01:00:51.760 --> 01:00:53.580
Dream. The Game Grumps fight the—

01:00:53.580 --> 01:00:56.000
Mr. Dream. For a half hour, but I didn't—

01:00:56.000 --> 01:00:58.420
Okay, you watched him fight him for a half hour?

01:00:58.620 --> 01:00:59.700
I mean, over and over again.

01:00:59.780 --> 01:01:00.820
Not one match, obviously.

01:01:00.960 --> 01:01:03.420
I watched some videos about fighting him, but I didn't fight him.

01:01:03.600 --> 01:01:04.940
Okay, so this is what I'll say.

01:01:05.100 --> 01:01:08.020
The only thing that I encountered with Mr.

01:01:08.140 --> 01:01:10.820
Dream was that I do not—

01:01:10.820 --> 01:01:15.900
And I do not know who does, but I do not have the reflexes to even respond to anything.

01:01:15.900 --> 01:01:20.660
I found myself just ducking all—like, not ducking to left and right, like, just blocking—

01:01:20.660 --> 01:01:21.500
Just cowering with a little baby.

01:01:21.500 --> 01:01:24.340
As much as I could, and then being like, oh, that looks like an opening.

01:01:24.360 --> 01:01:25.540
And then, nope, he blocked.

01:01:25.540 --> 01:01:27.240
Oh, that looks like a—nope, he blocks it.

01:01:27.380 --> 01:01:29.860
He blocked every single punch that I did.

01:01:29.980 --> 01:01:34.960
And granted, I only tried him three times, but if you suck so badly all three times, there's

01:01:34.960 --> 01:01:36.860
a good chance you're not saying, I'll take more of that.

01:01:37.000 --> 01:01:38.200
Yeah. I'll continue again.

01:01:38.420 --> 01:01:41.080
And I've watched videos, too, of people playing Mr.

01:01:41.200 --> 01:01:42.380
Dream and beating him, obviously.

01:01:42.380 --> 01:01:43.420
They put it in their title.

01:01:44.020 --> 01:01:45.840
And it's just like they're playing on another level.

01:01:46.200 --> 01:01:48.960
It's just not fair. It doesn't look as hard when you watch someone playing it.

01:01:48.960 --> 01:01:54.580
And it's the equivalent of watching someone else play—we've talked about this sometimes

01:01:54.580 --> 01:01:57.340
with NES games—where you watch someone else play, and you're like, oh, man, why aren't

01:01:57.340 --> 01:01:58.480
you doing that thing right now?

01:01:58.560 --> 01:02:01.380
And then you get in there, and you suck just as much.

01:02:01.480 --> 01:02:04.420
Like, we were doing that with Ghost and Goblins, me and you, and I was like, Joe, no, no, no,

01:02:04.460 --> 01:02:06.340
you gotta do this. I can't do it.

01:02:06.620 --> 01:02:07.580
You try it. Right.

01:02:07.780 --> 01:02:09.160
I go in, and I die even earlier.

01:02:09.440 --> 01:02:13.320
You know? It's like, there's this weird thing about the Mr.

01:02:13.460 --> 01:02:15.340
Dream fight that I feel you either

01:02:15.340 --> 01:02:16.560
have it or you don't.

01:02:16.820 --> 01:02:18.860
You're born with it or you'll never have it.

01:02:18.940 --> 01:02:21.180
Because I didn't even earn the fight.

01:02:21.320 --> 01:02:23.680
Because I was putting in a password to even get there.

01:02:23.860 --> 01:02:24.980
You weren't worthy. Yeah.

01:02:25.100 --> 01:02:27.500
I just didn't even have the skill set required.

01:02:28.700 --> 01:02:32.140
So in the video that I watched, it was mostly dodging.

01:02:32.300 --> 01:02:37.280
But then there must be some tell that I wasn't recognizing where after enough—at a certain

01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:39.960
point, he would get several punches in at once.

01:02:40.120 --> 01:02:41.640
Like, maybe like six at once.

01:02:41.700 --> 01:02:42.840
And that was like his pattern.

01:02:43.220 --> 01:02:47.380
And once in a while, there's like a split—it's like a frame.

01:02:47.640 --> 01:02:49.060
Mr. Dream. Cries.

01:02:49.500 --> 01:02:50.340
Oh. Yeah, cries.

01:02:50.620 --> 01:02:52.020
No. He goes for one frame.

01:02:52.020 --> 01:02:53.020
And you're right. It's one frame.

01:02:53.280 --> 01:02:55.960
It's one single frame. And if you put it on that, it's a knockout.

01:02:56.140 --> 01:03:02.060
Wow. The only thing that's funny about that, because I also watched this video, is when he glows

01:03:02.060 --> 01:03:06.520
for that one frame, if you do manage to punch him, he makes the stupidest face in the

01:03:06.520 --> 01:03:07.340
whole world.

01:03:07.500 --> 01:03:08.540
It is so funny.

01:03:08.940 --> 01:03:12.440
First off, it's funny that they even designed him to not look good.

01:03:12.640 --> 01:03:17.200
This game is named after him, and they already designed him as someone who's taken a few

01:03:17.200 --> 01:03:18.080
blows to the head.

01:03:18.300 --> 01:03:21.680
He's got a very puffy, cheeky face.

01:03:22.300 --> 01:03:23.400
And now you're just making fun of him.

01:03:23.520 --> 01:03:26.880
No, I'm just—I'm saying, I think sometimes he looked better in real life.

01:03:27.020 --> 01:03:28.000
Like, it's actually crazy.

01:03:28.420 --> 01:03:30.900
Even in the— Wait, you're saying that he looked better in real life than on the NES?

01:03:32.360 --> 01:03:33.720
Yes, I am saying that.

01:03:33.720 --> 01:03:39.920
All right. Even in the in-between rounds, you know, when you're in the corners, he does not look good

01:03:39.920 --> 01:03:41.480
in the corners. He looks like he's been bruised.

01:03:41.680 --> 01:03:43.160
One of his eyes barely opens.

01:03:43.320 --> 01:03:44.660
And it's not because you've injured him.

01:03:44.720 --> 01:03:46.320
It's just the way that they designed him.

01:03:46.580 --> 01:03:48.800
Is it because he's already got the facial tattoo, or?

01:03:49.900 --> 01:03:50.780
No, I'm joking.

01:03:50.780 --> 01:03:52.880
At this point, he does not have the facial tattoo.

01:03:53.100 --> 01:03:53.960
Nintendo predicted it.

01:03:54.200 --> 01:04:00.000
There you are. I did—I am managing to pull up the frame now, so just give me one second, and I'll

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:01.040
show you guys right now.

01:04:01.180 --> 01:04:04.860
I won't show you guys, but I'm going to show these guys, so I'll play some music during

01:04:04.860 --> 01:04:19.300
this. Wow, Mike, that music really captures the look on his face.

01:04:21.240 --> 01:04:23.860
Well, are you saying that the music sounded really dumb?

01:04:24.960 --> 01:04:28.780
I'm saying that I—while I was listening to that music, I wasn't even looking at the

01:04:28.780 --> 01:04:29.820
face, but I know what you mean.

01:04:29.920 --> 01:04:30.840
Oh, okay. Got it.

01:04:30.900 --> 01:04:33.160
Yes. I'm sure that's how the listeners at home feel, too.

01:04:33.160 --> 01:04:37.580
Yeah. So that's pretty much all I have to say about Punch-Out, and Mr.

01:04:37.700 --> 01:04:40.240
Dream. Does anybody else have anything to add?

01:04:42.400 --> 01:04:43.920
If—no. Yeah, I do.

01:04:44.140 --> 01:04:51.780
Okay, yeah, go for it. So I—before—or while playing this game, I checked out Summoning Salts.

01:04:52.360 --> 01:04:54.920
He was a—he's a speedrunner of this game.

01:04:55.080 --> 01:04:59.400
Oh, I've heard of him. He does the progression videos on YouTube as well.

01:04:59.400 --> 01:05:03.680
Yeah, yeah, yeah. He, like, documents how each game is evolved in speedrunning.

01:05:03.720 --> 01:05:08.960
And he does really good videos on these, and one of the videos he did was a blindfolded

01:05:08.960 --> 01:05:10.280
punch-out video.

01:05:10.280 --> 01:05:12.420
Which just puts everything I just said before into shit.

01:05:12.740 --> 01:05:19.840
And the history of, you know, people doing that who—I think they started doing it at AGDQ.

01:05:20.040 --> 01:05:22.220
Yes. Awesome games done quick for the uninformed.

01:05:22.360 --> 01:05:28.100
Yeah. And it's—it's wild to know that some people have beaten this game without even looking

01:05:28.100 --> 01:05:32.960
at it. Right. And—and that's when I think it really becomes a rhythm game, because you're just listening

01:05:32.960 --> 01:05:34.220
to music cues—

01:05:34.220 --> 01:05:35.600
Yeah. —to know when to punch people.

01:05:35.860 --> 01:05:39.240
And he also mentioned something in the video, which I don't think we talked about, which is

01:05:39.240 --> 01:05:43.940
when you get knocked down, I think if you wait until a certain amount of time, you

01:05:43.940 --> 01:05:45.560
get more health when you come back up.

01:05:45.780 --> 01:05:49.040
Oh, interesting. So if you wait till, like, the seven count—

01:05:49.040 --> 01:05:49.840
Something like that.

01:05:50.480 --> 01:05:52.520
Yeah. I like it.

01:05:52.800 --> 01:05:55.040
Yeah. But you guys—everyone should check out that channel, because it's really good.

01:05:55.380 --> 01:05:59.260
And then, Sean, this might have been something you either asked earlier in the podcast or

01:05:59.260 --> 01:06:01.420
before we started, or maybe it was Joe.

01:06:01.620 --> 01:06:07.580
I don't remember. One of you asked about the music for during the—

01:06:07.580 --> 01:06:13.060
Oh, yes. I recognized the music during the cut scenes as from something else.

01:06:13.160 --> 01:06:19.740
And that is—that song is called Look Sharp, Be Sharp, March by Mahan Merrick.

01:06:19.740 --> 01:06:25.360
And that theme was used for the radio and TV program Gillette Cavalcade of Sports, which

01:06:25.360 --> 01:06:29.100
covered a variety of different sports, but over time began to focus more on boxing.

01:06:29.380 --> 01:06:35.120
Prior to the release of Punch-Out in 1987, this song was featured in Raging Bull.

01:06:35.120 --> 01:06:40.080
Well, so I just knew this song from, like, real life and not video games?

01:06:40.220 --> 01:06:43.820
Yes. It just sounds so much like a video game song.

01:06:44.060 --> 01:06:47.500
It's interesting to think that this song was also in Raging Bull, but also makes it more

01:06:47.500 --> 01:06:48.700
perfect for Punch-Out.

01:06:48.840 --> 01:06:50.680
Raging Bull is the boxing movie.

01:06:51.020 --> 01:06:52.680
True. Except for Rocky.

01:06:53.260 --> 01:06:55.060
Yeah, I guess that's true, but I don't know.

01:06:55.120 --> 01:06:56.860
I think Raging Bull is better than Rocky.

01:06:56.920 --> 01:06:58.100
Well, it is. How does this song go again?

01:06:58.640 --> 01:07:04.660
Which one? I'm Tome Duff.

01:07:04.780 --> 01:07:05.820
I can't do it. No, that's good.

01:07:05.980 --> 01:07:06.860
That sounded right. Got it? Yeah.

01:07:07.020 --> 01:07:08.460
No, never mind. Cool.

01:07:09.080 --> 01:07:12.020
Then there's a lot of sequels and spinoffs to cover.

01:07:12.220 --> 01:07:13.360
Wait, I do have one more thing.

01:07:13.460 --> 01:07:15.120
Oh, okay, yeah. So when you beat Mr.

01:07:15.260 --> 01:07:17.680
Dream, which obviously none of us did, but when you beat Mr.

01:07:17.840 --> 01:07:22.660
Dream, you get a little cutscene with all of your opponents, kind of like a little—

01:07:22.660 --> 01:07:23.480
Group hug?

01:07:23.780 --> 01:07:27.460
No, no. They each have their own little profile picture that comes up.

01:07:27.640 --> 01:07:29.540
Okay. But then you also get a newspaper.

01:07:30.020 --> 01:07:31.480
And can I read a little bit of this newspaper?

01:07:31.480 --> 01:07:33.660
Oh, yes, yes. Please talk about the newspaper because it's really good.

01:07:33.800 --> 01:07:35.220
It's really good. I'm glad you brought this up.

01:07:35.240 --> 01:07:36.800
So we've got a newspaper and there's Little Mac.

01:07:37.000 --> 01:07:38.540
You know, he's got the champion belt on.

01:07:38.620 --> 01:07:39.780
His arms are up. He's triumphant.

01:07:39.920 --> 01:07:42.540
And it says, New champ, the WVBA World Circuit.

01:07:42.760 --> 01:07:44.780
Last night we found a small but great champ.

01:07:44.980 --> 01:07:46.100
His name is Little Mac.

01:07:46.380 --> 01:07:47.540
Push start for the dream fight.

01:07:47.620 --> 01:07:48.860
Okay, so I'm sorry. This is before.

01:07:49.420 --> 01:07:51.460
This is before the dream fight.

01:07:51.660 --> 01:07:52.840
Push start for the dream fight.

01:07:52.940 --> 01:07:53.800
It's printed in the newspaper.

01:07:54.020 --> 01:07:56.580
Then underneath, there's like—I guess these are unrelated.

01:07:56.580 --> 01:07:59.800
There's like these ads that were taken out that they included in the newspaper.

01:08:00.060 --> 01:08:02.220
The first one, Daddy, come back home.

01:08:02.440 --> 01:08:04.100
No, with a picture of Mario.

01:08:04.900 --> 01:08:06.780
I don't know if it's Mario, but—

01:08:06.780 --> 01:08:08.000
Oh, a picture of the rat.

01:08:08.320 --> 01:08:12.520
No, but Daddy, come back home is actually in reference to Glass Joe, I believe.

01:08:12.880 --> 01:08:18.960
Really? Yeah. I don't know the whole story on this, but I heard something about that newspaper with—I

01:08:18.960 --> 01:08:21.120
mean, like, this is all hearsay at this point, but I heard that—

01:08:21.120 --> 01:08:22.400
It also has a phone number.

01:08:22.500 --> 01:08:24.020
Yeah. Oh, is there a—

01:08:24.020 --> 01:08:25.360
It says, I found a key.

01:08:25.560 --> 01:08:27.360
Oh, yeah. So that looks like it's unrelated.

01:08:27.360 --> 01:08:28.320
I found a key.

01:08:28.740 --> 01:08:29.640
Maybe that's a code.

01:08:29.780 --> 01:08:31.780
Is that a code? Did anybody call that number?

01:08:31.920 --> 01:08:33.100
I didn't call that. Back in the day?

01:08:33.100 --> 01:08:34.940
I didn't call it. What if we call it now?

01:08:35.640 --> 01:08:36.920
Let's call it. Right here on the air.

01:08:37.280 --> 01:08:38.140
We're on the air, right?

01:08:38.420 --> 01:08:39.420
Yeah, we're on the air.

01:08:40.340 --> 01:08:41.160
This is live.

01:08:41.940 --> 01:08:46.040
Yeah, I don't—I thought that Daddy, come back home thing was related to something.

01:08:46.160 --> 01:08:47.640
That's terrifying. Unfortunately, I do not know.

01:08:50.640 --> 01:08:51.460
So there's that.

01:08:51.880 --> 01:08:53.420
Yeah, and the date is April 1st.

01:08:53.940 --> 01:08:55.540
So you didn't really beat him.

01:08:55.720 --> 01:08:56.920
This is an all-April fools joke.

01:08:57.020 --> 01:08:58.700
Yeah. Oh, yeah, that's true.

01:08:58.720 --> 01:08:59.840
It is April 1st.

01:08:59.880 --> 01:09:01.360
Wow. Okay, there's a lot of things about this game going on.

01:09:01.360 --> 01:09:03.840
They make a joke about that in the Game Grumps, and I thought it was funny.

01:09:04.060 --> 01:09:06.620
No 17-year-old 107-pound kid could beat Mr.

01:09:06.780 --> 01:09:08.360
Dream. You've been punked.

01:09:13.660 --> 01:09:16.040
All right. Sequels and spinoffs time.

01:09:16.320 --> 01:09:18.680
Here we go. Super Punch-Out!

01:09:19.300 --> 01:09:22.720
Comes out in arcades before this game.

01:09:24.140 --> 01:09:25.640
Oh. So Punch-Out!

01:09:25.680 --> 01:09:29.360
is released in 1987 on the NES, but Super Punch-Out!

01:09:29.480 --> 01:09:31.560
comes out in the arcade in 1985.

01:09:32.160 --> 01:09:33.880
So this is like a demake?

01:09:34.580 --> 01:09:40.300
Right? In some ways, it's like we cannot possibly port the Super Punch-Out!

01:09:40.340 --> 01:09:42.340
version of this game to the NES.

01:09:42.520 --> 01:09:44.140
So we'll just give them the regular Punch-Out!

01:09:45.020 --> 01:09:46.300
So, hold on.

01:09:46.780 --> 01:09:48.120
The order goes Punch-Out!

01:09:48.160 --> 01:09:49.080
for arcade in 84.

01:09:49.460 --> 01:09:50.420
Super Punch-Out!

01:09:50.480 --> 01:09:51.840
in 85. Punch-Out!

01:09:51.880 --> 01:09:55.660
in 87. Then Super Punch-Out!

01:09:55.680 --> 01:09:57.800
for Super Nintendo in 1994.

01:09:57.800 --> 01:10:00.920
And then Punch-Out!

01:10:00.980 --> 01:10:07.700
goes like dark for a really long time before it returns in 2009 for the Wii.

01:10:08.300 --> 01:10:12.240
Yes. And they took on a really cool style for that game.

01:10:12.420 --> 01:10:15.180
I don't want to say cel-shaded, but definitely like cartoon-ish.

01:10:15.420 --> 01:10:16.340
Cartoon-ish. Yeah.

01:10:16.540 --> 01:10:21.960
And you use the Wiimote and the Nunchuck as your one-two punch kind of thing.

01:10:22.080 --> 01:10:28.460
And then you can even use the balance board to sway to the left and right to dodge.

01:10:28.660 --> 01:10:31.360
I think that that's a pretty unique stance for the...

01:10:31.360 --> 01:10:33.120
I mean, like how many other games is the balance board working?

01:10:33.120 --> 01:10:34.340
It's basically VR. Yeah.

01:10:34.700 --> 01:10:37.380
I always felt like every Wii game was VR.

01:10:39.640 --> 01:10:42.200
And then if that wasn't weird enough, Punch-Out!

01:10:42.220 --> 01:10:44.900
making its like random return in 2009 for the Wii,

01:10:45.580 --> 01:10:47.140
Doc Lewis' Punch-Out!

01:10:47.620 --> 01:10:49.580
gets released for WiiWare.

01:10:50.000 --> 01:10:52.300
Do you take the role as a corner man?

01:10:52.560 --> 01:10:53.460
It's just a...

01:10:53.460 --> 01:10:55.340
No, you just...

01:10:55.340 --> 01:10:56.400
It's a training game.

01:10:56.600 --> 01:10:57.820
It's like a training mode.

01:10:58.120 --> 01:11:01.400
Oh, I see. It's a standalone prequel to Punch-Out!

01:11:01.400 --> 01:11:05.160
Punch-Out! Where you're essentially just training for the minor circuit.

01:11:05.480 --> 01:11:06.860
Huh. Wow.

01:11:07.120 --> 01:11:09.060
Yep. So wait, what do you do in that game?

01:11:09.240 --> 01:11:12.920
You're just doing like practice essentially with...

01:11:12.920 --> 01:11:13.880
It's just...

01:11:13.880 --> 01:11:19.680
So for those that don't know, WiiWare was like what they called these downloadable,

01:11:19.980 --> 01:11:23.000
like very cheap games that you could only get on the Wii Shop.

01:11:23.400 --> 01:11:24.680
Okay. For like...

01:11:24.680 --> 01:11:28.160
And it's only for a limited time too, so you couldn't actually get Doc Lewis' Punch-Out!

01:11:28.260 --> 01:11:31.880
anymore. There's no way to play that game other than via Dolphin, I guess.

01:11:32.220 --> 01:11:38.220
Interesting. And so in that game, you were essentially just doing like target practice and maybe like a fight or two.

01:11:38.820 --> 01:11:41.300
It was not a full game with a story mode and everything.

01:11:41.560 --> 01:11:46.060
Wow. Yep. Then Little Mac of course joins the fight in Super Smash Bros.

01:11:46.680 --> 01:11:48.500
for Wii U and 3DS.

01:11:49.200 --> 01:11:50.960
And Sam as our resident Smash Bros.

01:11:51.060 --> 01:11:53.100
guy, how do you feel about Little Mac in Smash Bros.?

01:11:53.100 --> 01:11:57.560
I personally don't like fighting him, but you know, he's just a new character, so you gotta get used to it.

01:11:57.800 --> 01:11:58.760
And I think that it's...

01:11:58.760 --> 01:11:59.580
I think that he's...

01:11:59.580 --> 01:12:00.460
It's a really cool addition.

01:12:00.640 --> 01:12:04.380
I think that he suffers a lot in Smash Ultimate because he has no recovery.

01:12:04.840 --> 01:12:07.100
Yeah. Is he new to Ultimate?

01:12:07.420 --> 01:12:09.280
No. He's new to Smash 4.

01:12:09.480 --> 01:12:11.620
Yeah. I don't like that he has the...

01:12:11.620 --> 01:12:12.800
I forget what it's called.

01:12:13.060 --> 01:12:14.840
The... The KO?

01:12:14.940 --> 01:12:15.740
The KO punch.

01:12:16.060 --> 01:12:17.400
The uppercut thing.

01:12:17.580 --> 01:12:19.060
Okay. You just press B and someone dies.

01:12:19.060 --> 01:12:20.600
I don't like that, but that's just me.

01:12:21.180 --> 01:12:22.280
It was a real...

01:12:22.280 --> 01:12:25.640
So that is a little confusing though, but that was in the Punch-Out!

01:12:25.680 --> 01:12:27.060
arcade game. Okay.

01:12:27.060 --> 01:12:31.020
There is a meter that builds up as you play well in Punch-Out!

01:12:31.140 --> 01:12:34.340
Yeah. I'm just not crazy about that as a mechanic in Smash Bros.

01:12:34.560 --> 01:12:38.520
How faithful do you feel like he translates over though from playing Punch-Out!

01:12:38.600 --> 01:12:43.960
to this? I feel like... I feel like now I have to go back and play as Little Mac in both of these games to really

01:12:43.960 --> 01:12:46.360
see because I think that he is really...

01:12:46.360 --> 01:12:48.040
I think it's more like a spirit of Little Mac.

01:12:48.120 --> 01:12:52.100
Yeah. It doesn't really like translate how you play as Little Mac in Punch-Out!

01:12:52.160 --> 01:12:55.520
I'm thinking about the moves because his side B is like the...

01:12:55.520 --> 01:12:58.680
It's like the star punch, you know, where he lunges.

01:12:58.960 --> 01:13:05.060
Yeah. The aerial moves are kind of whatever because it's not really anything.

01:13:05.600 --> 01:13:09.220
Yeah. It's mostly just punches though, which is nice.

01:13:09.420 --> 01:13:11.900
He's little and he's fast, which I guess is what...

01:13:12.360 --> 01:13:15.740
I just think it's nice they didn't give him anything like stupid, you know?

01:13:15.860 --> 01:13:17.220
They didn't give him a sword or a gun.

01:13:17.320 --> 01:13:19.980
Right, exactly. Or like any accessories.

01:13:20.380 --> 01:13:21.680
Yeah. Imagine if he just like...

01:13:21.680 --> 01:13:25.620
I like his outfits. Well, imagine if he just like threw like one of those boxing bag things, you know?

01:13:25.700 --> 01:13:28.820
Oh, yeah. It's like if he brought one of those out, like that's dumb.

01:13:28.980 --> 01:13:31.940
It would have been more like the design of Duck Hunt Dog.

01:13:32.360 --> 01:13:35.240
Yeah. He's like working with...

01:13:35.240 --> 01:13:37.120
Mr. Dream. He's like lifting him up and stuff.

01:13:38.280 --> 01:13:40.460
That's funny. I like that.

01:13:40.780 --> 01:13:47.180
This is why we talk. I think as far as, you know, a Smash Brothers version of him, I think they did a pretty

01:13:47.180 --> 01:13:48.220
good job. Cool.

01:13:48.380 --> 01:13:50.040
See, for me as someone who doesn't...

01:13:50.040 --> 01:13:50.980
I'm not a Smash Brothers guy.

01:13:51.000 --> 01:13:52.520
I'm pretty bad at the game.

01:13:52.640 --> 01:13:57.460
But I do... When I do play, I always kind of play around with different characters and see...

01:13:57.460 --> 01:14:00.500
Once in a while, I'll do okay with a character and I'll be like, oh, that's going to be my

01:14:00.500 --> 01:14:01.340
character from now on.

01:14:01.420 --> 01:14:02.400
It's really just a fluke.

01:14:04.100 --> 01:14:06.920
But Little Mac was one of those in Smash Brothers Ultimate.

01:14:06.920 --> 01:14:08.860
And I was doing really good with him.

01:14:09.240 --> 01:14:12.480
But I do agree with you about the one-hit KO punch.

01:14:12.700 --> 01:14:13.520
It's kind of cheap.

01:14:14.080 --> 01:14:19.200
But without really remembering too much of how he played, because I'm not a big Smash

01:14:19.200 --> 01:14:21.720
Brothers guy in general, I really liked playing as him.

01:14:22.100 --> 01:14:28.520
Yeah. I think he's fun and I think he's good for beginners to get a good upper hand on people

01:14:28.520 --> 01:14:29.820
because he does a ton of damage.

01:14:30.040 --> 01:14:35.920
So as long as you're willing to go out there and connect some punches, you'll punch out your

01:14:35.920 --> 01:14:37.660
opponent. Ha! Name drop.

01:14:38.020 --> 01:14:43.920
We did it. Yep. It is time now for a little game we like to call The Essential Games List.

01:14:46.920 --> 01:14:52.200
Guys, today we are voting on Punch Out for the unaware listener who just listened to about

01:14:52.200 --> 01:14:56.600
an hour of us talking about it and is now like, oh, they're voting on Punch Out?

01:14:56.780 --> 01:14:57.640
Not Smash.

01:14:57.820 --> 01:14:58.660
Not Smash, yeah.

01:14:58.840 --> 01:15:00.520
We will get there some year.

01:15:00.720 --> 01:15:02.600
Yeah. 3,060 or something.

01:15:03.000 --> 01:15:04.820
But right now, we're just talking about Punch Out.

01:15:04.820 --> 01:15:07.540
We're voting whether it is essential today.

01:15:07.820 --> 01:15:09.220
Not talking about 1987.

01:15:09.220 --> 01:15:11.720
Not talking about that 1984 arcade port.

01:15:11.960 --> 01:15:15.140
I'm talking about, is this game still worth playing today?

01:15:15.380 --> 01:15:16.400
Does it hold up?

01:15:16.840 --> 01:15:21.420
Sam. I think this game is essential.

01:15:21.800 --> 01:15:23.000
I'm going to say it right off the bat.

01:15:23.260 --> 01:15:24.640
I think that there's... The wall-in baby.

01:15:24.800 --> 01:15:25.620
There's no...

01:15:25.620 --> 01:15:30.280
I think this is one of the most essential games for the NES that we've played.

01:15:30.280 --> 01:15:35.320
I think that playing this game, there's really no other game like it.

01:15:35.600 --> 01:15:36.920
It has all the...

01:15:36.920 --> 01:15:40.880
Like, the 8-bit graphics are charming.

01:15:41.180 --> 01:15:43.060
You know, it doesn't look weird or anything.

01:15:43.300 --> 01:15:48.260
Everyone has, like, a weird personality to their characters.

01:15:48.840 --> 01:15:53.200
The mechanics are simple, but they're really, you know, easy to figure out.

01:15:53.200 --> 01:15:56.280
But it's complicated to beat all the characters.

01:15:56.660 --> 01:15:57.820
You know, it's got music.

01:15:57.820 --> 01:16:03.740
It's got that killer cutscene when you're running and possibly what is Staten Island, maybe Battery Park.

01:16:03.860 --> 01:16:05.540
We're not really sure. And...

01:16:05.540 --> 01:16:06.960
This is very important.

01:16:08.900 --> 01:16:10.040
And it's hard.

01:16:10.280 --> 01:16:12.820
It's that kind of Nintendo hard that you hear about.

01:16:13.020 --> 01:16:14.340
But I think that it's a...

01:16:14.340 --> 01:16:16.060
Like we talked about, I think it's a fair hard.

01:16:16.060 --> 01:16:21.460
I think it is an essential NES game and just an essential game in general.

01:16:22.000 --> 01:16:23.100
Great. Joe?

01:16:23.340 --> 01:16:29.220
So I came into this with these two conflicting notions about this game.

01:16:29.380 --> 01:16:34.560
One was my, so far, previous experience with games that are trying to simulate some sort of fighting.

01:16:34.700 --> 01:16:38.700
Boxing or wrestling. Which have been very bad so far up until this point.

01:16:39.020 --> 01:16:44.320
But the other was knowing that this is a really popular, famous game that everyone's nostalgic for.

01:16:44.320 --> 01:16:51.280
So, like I said in the beginning, I kind of thought that this was going to be like one of those games that people misremember as being good.

01:16:51.380 --> 01:16:52.280
And it's really not that great.

01:16:52.340 --> 01:16:59.560
But I was pleasantly surprised to find this is, I think, one of the best games we've played so far, gameplay-wise.

01:16:59.760 --> 01:17:04.200
And I think especially because of the way that you learn a boss.

01:17:04.380 --> 01:17:10.000
I think that what we were talking about, the boss rush element of it, is what really makes it stand out to me.

01:17:10.000 --> 01:17:14.540
That it feels like a satisfying experience for each battle.

01:17:14.720 --> 01:17:16.820
Especially the more difficult ones that come later on.

01:17:17.200 --> 01:17:19.000
To have struggled with it for a while.

01:17:19.180 --> 01:17:20.900
Slowly learn it. Understand the patterns.

01:17:21.160 --> 01:17:25.840
And that moment when you finally beat it is just so, there's something so satisfying about it.

01:17:25.860 --> 01:17:27.860
Similar to the way, you know, games are now.

01:17:28.540 --> 01:17:30.040
Sorry, I'm getting a little long-winded here.

01:17:30.080 --> 01:17:31.780
No, it's okay. It's very beautiful.

01:17:32.320 --> 01:17:36.280
And Sam mentioned the Nintendo hard idea.

01:17:36.480 --> 01:17:42.160
And I think this is that Nintendo hard, but not the negative side.

01:17:42.400 --> 01:17:47.120
Not the jank hard. Yeah, not the negative side of Nintendo hard where it's just, I mean, I'm kind of just mirroring what you just said.

01:17:47.240 --> 01:17:50.780
But where it's just bad game design.

01:17:50.780 --> 01:17:55.840
It's hard where it's a challenge that you can learn and you can actually get better at it.

01:17:56.480 --> 01:17:57.920
So, no, not essential.

01:17:59.700 --> 01:18:00.580
Definitely essential.

01:18:00.580 --> 01:18:05.940
Okay, if Sean votes it in, then it will be an essential game.

01:18:06.180 --> 01:18:12.040
Sean? Alright, so I come into this again struggling.

01:18:12.320 --> 01:18:18.040
Not struggling, but wrestling with the idea of what the essential games list means.

01:18:18.640 --> 01:18:25.980
And when it comes to Punch-Out, we already know that there is a Super Punch-Out.

01:18:26.380 --> 01:18:29.740
I haven't really played too much of Super Punch-Out.

01:18:29.740 --> 01:18:34.560
So, I don't know if it is just a flat-out better version of this game.

01:18:34.560 --> 01:18:42.620
So, I have to think about Punch-Out as the only Punch-Out that I've ever played.

01:18:43.980 --> 01:18:53.680
But regardless of whether one's better than the other, I think that the series as a whole itself is just a unique enough experience.

01:18:54.520 --> 01:18:59.600
But regardless of whether one game is better than the other within the same series.

01:19:00.060 --> 01:19:09.020
Since I've only played one, and I agree with everything that Sam and Joe has said about it in regards to how good this game is, so I won't reiterate.

01:19:09.440 --> 01:19:14.160
I think that this game is also essential, even if Super Punch-Out may be better.

01:19:14.280 --> 01:19:17.840
I haven't played it. There's a lot of things that make a game non-essential.

01:19:18.200 --> 01:19:26.480
I think I check off more boxes right off the bat when I start a game that make it not essential than would ever make me consider it being essential.

01:19:26.760 --> 01:19:33.700
I actually, when I judge these games, I actually think, why wouldn't this game be on the essential games list rather than why it should be on?

01:19:34.320 --> 01:19:40.600
Then there's certain games that are Nintendo-made games that are very well-known games in the history of video games.

01:19:40.740 --> 01:19:46.120
We've dealt with this a few times now with Super Mario Bros., Legend of Zelda, Metroid, and now Punch-Out.

01:19:46.140 --> 01:19:55.120
These are the four games that I think a lot of people just know that they're essential games, and it's up to us to kind of put our own spin on that.

01:19:55.300 --> 01:19:58.680
Even if you suspected that we were going to vote Super Mario Bros.

01:19:58.920 --> 01:20:01.300
in as essential, or The Legend of Zelda in as essential.

01:20:02.600 --> 01:20:06.200
Sometimes you have played those games before, sometimes you haven't.

01:20:06.380 --> 01:20:08.540
I didn't play Punch-Out before we started doing this.

01:20:08.640 --> 01:20:11.960
I honestly didn't even see too much of that game beforehand.

01:20:11.960 --> 01:20:14.660
I didn't know how to play.

01:20:14.660 --> 01:20:16.880
I didn't see any videos that often.

01:20:17.200 --> 01:20:28.560
So entering this game for me was just a surprise in general because after playing for maybe four to five hours, I thought about what would I want to change in this game.

01:20:28.560 --> 01:20:33.840
And the things that I want to change in this game are not gameplay related.

01:20:34.140 --> 01:20:41.920
They're more like just quality of life improvements like a practice mode or a save battery or maybe...

01:20:41.920 --> 01:20:43.060
You want more of this game.

01:20:43.180 --> 01:20:44.020
Yeah, exactly.

01:20:44.600 --> 01:20:50.680
Maybe an option to choose who you fight next instead of just getting thrown into the same bracket each time.

01:20:51.460 --> 01:20:58.240
There's something about that component of it where I'm asking for more features but not necessarily like,

01:20:58.620 --> 01:21:01.080
Oh, I wish that punching mechanics would have worked this way.

01:21:01.080 --> 01:21:03.580
Or I wish that you did have the freedom to move.

01:21:03.700 --> 01:21:09.520
Or I wish that Little Mac was bigger in scale so I could do other things to my opponent.

01:21:09.780 --> 01:21:12.460
It doesn't matter because I'm not wishing for those things.

01:21:12.500 --> 01:21:15.720
And I think that that's the testament of an essential game.

01:21:15.720 --> 01:21:25.040
To what Sean was talking about when it comes to Super Punch-Out, the only thing I'll say there is I don't have a problem with Punch-Out and Super Punch-Out being on an essential games list.

01:21:25.040 --> 01:21:27.680
Just like I wouldn't have a problem with Super Mario Bros.

01:21:27.760 --> 01:21:29.300
1 and possibly Super Mario Bros.

01:21:29.380 --> 01:21:33.120
2, 3, World, all those other games being on the essential games list.

01:21:33.320 --> 01:21:36.400
Each game at the end of the day still speaks for itself.

01:21:36.960 --> 01:21:43.680
And I think that there is a difference between playing, say, a Pokemon Red and Blue over playing Pokemon Sun and Moon.

01:21:43.860 --> 01:21:48.540
You can say that Pokemon Sun and Moon are a million times better because they have a million more times content.

01:21:49.520 --> 01:21:55.380
But at the end of the day there's a certain atmosphere and polish to Red and Blue that Sun and Moon aren't going to offer.

01:21:55.420 --> 01:21:56.360
And that's the same thing here.

01:21:56.540 --> 01:22:02.540
There's a Wii game that takes Punch-Out and completely flips it with a Wiimote and a Nunchuck.

01:22:02.540 --> 01:22:06.800
And you're actually controlling the character and that sounds like an awesome boxing experience.

01:22:06.800 --> 01:22:08.840
But does it translate to a great game?

01:22:09.140 --> 01:22:10.540
I don't know. I didn't play it.

01:22:10.620 --> 01:22:14.240
But I did play Mike Tyson's Punch-Out on the NES.

01:22:14.460 --> 01:22:17.100
And I'm confidently going to vote that into the essential games list.

01:22:17.300 --> 01:22:24.680
Not only because I think that's where it belongs just based on its ability to play so well in 2019.

01:22:24.680 --> 01:22:35.900
But also just because the amount of polish it has over virtually every NES game we've played so far besides, for me, Gradius is just insane.

01:22:36.180 --> 01:22:38.980
I think this game is way more polished than, say, Super Mario Brothers.

01:22:39.400 --> 01:22:40.380
It was beautiful, Mike.

01:22:46.640 --> 01:22:56.100
And when I say polished for Gradius because I know I'm going to get some flack for that, I really want people to think about all of the options and choices you have in Gradius.

01:22:56.220 --> 01:22:59.960
And I think that that's really, like, something that NES games just weren't offering.

01:23:00.180 --> 01:23:03.260
Legend of Zelda offers you an exploration that no other game can offer.

01:23:03.260 --> 01:23:10.840
But there are still some gameplay tweaks there that definitely could have been made where, say, Gradius, just no matter how hard it is, it's still –

01:23:10.840 --> 01:23:14.080
It is Gradius. Yeah, it's still – it's great ES.

01:23:15.420 --> 01:23:19.080
So I think it's games like that and Legend of Zelda's on the essential games list.

01:23:19.100 --> 01:23:20.860
It's games like that that stand out for me.

01:23:20.940 --> 01:23:28.500
You know, we play maybe 10 to 11 bad games in a row and then all of a sudden we get fed one that's just like, wow, I definitely still want to play this.

01:23:28.660 --> 01:23:30.640
That makes the rest of the shit games worth it.

01:23:30.760 --> 01:23:32.980
Right. One day I'd love to beat Mr.

01:23:33.100 --> 01:23:36.860
Dream. I don't know if I ever will, but I definitely see this as a game I'll keep coming back to.

01:23:37.020 --> 01:23:38.660
I do go back to Balloon Fight.

01:23:38.880 --> 01:23:41.540
You know, that's a game that I voted essential and I still play it.

01:23:41.600 --> 01:23:43.340
I still try to beat my high scores on that.

01:23:43.480 --> 01:23:45.380
That's interesting because I'm not really a high score guy.

01:23:45.900 --> 01:23:46.940
So I think –

01:23:46.940 --> 01:23:48.700
I feel like we just learned something about you.

01:23:48.760 --> 01:23:57.540
Yeah, there's a thing to me about the essential games list where I'm confident in my votes because I keep going back to those games.

01:23:57.540 --> 01:24:04.940
I felt like if I vote something in and I'm not feeling it, there is a chance that I'm going to ask for it to be removed.

01:24:06.040 --> 01:24:09.160
All right. That's a thinly veiled threat, but I get it.

